!\\\\n./collab/1790479180000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2-fixes-r2.glm-flash.report.md:4:- `remote.cljc:281-300` — `send-request!`: on `:dao.stream/ok` it registers outstanding (registered-sends-only preserved) and clears the id from `:pending`; on a `:dao.stream/full` refusal of a descriptor probe it keeps the request in `:pending` instead of dropping it. Cursor/next/append! full refusals still leave nothing outstanding and nothing pending, exactly as in fix round 1.\\\\n./collab/1790479180000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2-fixes-r2.glm-flash.report.md:5:- `remote.cljc:458-464` — new `retry-pending!`: one more try of each kept probe per call.\\\\n./collab/1790479180000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2-fixes-r2.glm-flash.report.md:6:- `remote.cljc:467-481` — `drain!` calls `retry-pending!` once per drain (same cadence as the outstanding-probe ask counting), so after writer recovery the kept probe is re-sent; on acceptance it becomes the outstanding probe and the normal filing path (`absorb!` → `learn!`/`emit!`) confirms it.\\\\n./collab/1790479180000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2-fixes-r2.glm-flash.report.md:7:- `remote.cljc:659-678` — `refl-close` also forgets this reflection's kept probe, so nothing crosses for a closed reflection (preserving round-1 close semantics).\\\\n./collab/1790479180000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2-fixes-r2.glm-flash.report.md:10:- New gate test `a-refused-probe-is-kept-and-retried-on-a-later-drain` (:616): attach through a writer refusing 2 sends — the probe is refused and kept; the first drain's one retry is refused too (0 descriptor requests on the wire, no event); a later drain retries once and it crosses (= 1, proving once-per-drain); after one `serve!` the answer is filed the normal way (confirmation event with identity, ok, surface #{:reader}) and the learned surface answers `append!` as no-surface locally with nothing crossing.\\\\n./collab/1790479176072-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-confirm.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:14:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_5\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc | sed -n '1,620p'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"     1\\\\\\\\t(ns dao.stream.remote\\\\\\\\n     2\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"DaoStream remote: any stream, reachable as itself\\\\\\\\n     3\\\\\\\\t   (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md, sections 2 through 2.5).\\\\\\\\n     4\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n     5\\\\\\\\t   A peer that holds a handle answers the handle's own operations as\\\\\\\\n     6\\\\\\\\t   data over a channel; a peer at the other end holds a handle -- the\\\\\\\\n     7\\\\\\\\t   reflection -- whose operations are those questions asked across the\\\\\\\\n     8\\\\\\\\t   channel, with the source's outcome maps returned verbatim.\\\\\\\\n     9\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    10\\\\\\\\t   `mirror-step` is the pure answering step a peer runs over a channel\\\\\\\\n    11\\\\\\\\t   against its table. It never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor,\\\\\\\\n    12\\\\\\\\t   an anchor or an outcome, and its steps 1 to 4 are its only contacts\\\\\\\\n    13\\\\\\\\t   with the entry's handle: every cursor, next and append! runs through\\\\\\\\n    14\\\\\\\\t   `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request` with the channel context, so\\\\\\\\n    15\\\\\\\\t   nothing bypasses the entry's middleware or its declared surface.\\\\\\\\n    16\\\\\\\\t   `attacher` is the composition's :dao.stream/attach entry for\\\\\\\\n    17\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream/remote descriptors; it keeps one link per channel\\\\\\\\n    18\\\\\\\\t   descriptor, shared by every reflection through that channel, and\\\\\\\\n    19\\\\\\\\t   answers ok at once -- the contract's deferred remote confirmation.\\\\\\\\n    20\\\\\\\\t   There is no :dao.stream/create entry: attaching through this\\\\\\\\n    21\\\\\\\\t   transport never creates.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    22\\\\\\\\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\\\\\\\\n    23\\\\\\\\t            [dao.stream.middleware :as middleware]))\\\\\\\\n    24\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    25\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    26\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n    27\\\\\\\\t;; Wire shapes (2.1)\\\\\\\\n    28\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n    29\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    30\\\\\\\\t(def ^:private wire-ops\\\\\\\\n    31\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The four wire operations. close! never crosses -- the mirror holds\\\\\\\\n    32\\\\\\\\t   nothing per remote party to close and the owner's close is never\\\\\\\\n    33\\\\\\\\t   remote -- and attach! never crosses, because it consumes a\\\\\\\\n    34\\\\\\\\t   descriptor and produces a local handle.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    35\\\\\\\\t  #{:dao.stream/descriptor :dao.stream/cursor :dao.stream/next\\\\\\\\n    36\\\\\\\\t    :dao.stream/append!})\\\\\\\\n    37\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    38\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    39\\\\\\\\t(def ^:private protocol-errors\\\\\\\\n    40\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The three protocol errors, one answer shape for every operation,\\\\\\\\n    41\\\\\\\\t   a key instead of an outcome map because no operation's outcome set\\\\\\\\n    42\\\\\\\\t   can carry them.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    43\\\\\\\\t  #{:dao.stream.remote/not-found\\\\\\\\n    44\\\\\\\\t    :dao.stream.remote/no-surface\\\\\\\\n    45\\\\\\\\t    :dao.stream.remote/oversize})\\\\\\\\n    46\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    47\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    48\\\\\\\\t(defn- well-formed-request?\\\\\\\\n    49\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"True when `v` is a well-formed request: a map naming its identity\\\\\\\\n    50\\\\\\\\t   and one of the four wire ops, carrying an args vector and the\\\\\\\\n    51\\\\\\\\t   asker-minted id. Anything else is dropped as malformed wire input.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    52\\\\\\\\t  [v]\\\\\\\\n    53\\\\\\\\t  (and (map? v)\\\\\\\\n    54\\\\\\\\t       (contains? v :dao.stream/identity)\\\\\\\\n    55\\\\\\\\t       (contains? wire-ops (:dao.stream.remote/op v))\\\\\\\\n    56\\\\\\\\t       (vector? (:dao.stream.remote/args v))\\\\\\\\n    57\\\\\\\\t       (contains? v :dao.stream.remote/id)))\\\\\\\\n    58\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    59\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    60\\\\\\\\t(defn- well-formed-answer?\\\\\\\\n    61\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"True when `v` is a well-formed answer: a map carrying the id and\\\\\\\\n    62\\\\\\\\t   the identity and either a protocol error or an outcome map.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    63\\\\\\\\t  [v]\\\\\\\\n    64\\\\\\\\t  (and (map? v)\\\\\\\\n    65\\\\\\\\t       (contains? v :dao.stream.remote/id)\\\\\\\\n    66\\\\\\\\t       (contains? v :dao.stream/identity)\\\\\\\\n    67\\\\\\\\t       (or (contains? protocol-errors (:dao.stream.remote/error v))\\\\\\\\n    68\\\\\\\\t           (stream/outcome-map? v))))\\\\\\\\n    69\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    70\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    71\\\\\\\\t(defn- required-surface\\\\\\\\n    72\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The declared surface `op` needs, under the fixed mapping: cursor\\\\\\\\n    73\\\\\\\\t   and next need :reader, append! needs :writer, descriptor needs no\\\\\\\\n    74\\\\\\\\t   surface and is always answerable.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    75\\\\\\\\t  [op]\\\\\\\\n    76\\\\\\\\t  (case op\\\\\\\\n    77\\\\\\\\t    (:dao.stream/cursor :dao.stream/next) :reader\\\\\\\\n    78\\\\\\\\t    :dao.stream/append! :writer\\\\\\\\n    79\\\\\\\\t    nil))\\\\\\\\n    80\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    81\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    82\\\\\\\\t(defn- error-answer\\\\\\\\n    83\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The protocol-error answer shape, with the id and identity the\\\\\\\\n    84\\\\\\\\t   request carried.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    85\\\\\\\\t  [identity id e]\\\\\\\\n    86\\\\\\\\t  {:dao.stream/identity identity\\\\\\\\n    87\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream.remote/id id\\\\\\\\n    88\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream.remote/error e})\\\\\\\\n    89\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    90\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    91\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n    92\\\\\\\\t;; The mirror step (2.3)\\\\\\\\n    93\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n    94\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n    95\\\\\\\\t(defn- valid-budget?\\\\\\\\n    96\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"True when the request carries the optional next-only budget as a\\\\\\\\n    97\\\\\\\\t   positive integer. Anything else ignores the budget, which is always\\\\\\\\n    98\\\\\\\\t   correct for a mirror.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    99\\\\\\\\t  [req]\\\\\\\\n   100\\\\\\\\t  (let [k (:dao.stream.remote/budget req)]\\\\\\\\n   101\\\\\\\\t    (and (integer? k) (pos? k))))\\\\\\\\n   102\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   103\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   104\\\\\\\\t(defn- chase\\\\\\\\n   105\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The budget chase: `outcome` is an ok from next; follow the successor\\\\\\\\n   106\\\\\\\\t   cursor through the same apply-request path at most k - 1 further\\\\\\\\n   107\\\\\\\\t   times, collecting the further outcome maps in order. The vector ends\\\\\\\\n   108\\\\\\\\t   at and includes the first non-ok outcome, so blocked, end and gap\\\\\\\\n   109\\\\\\\\t   reach the reader with the source's own recovery cursor.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   110\\\\\\\\t  [h ctx k outcome]\\\\\\\\n   111\\\\\\\\t  (loop [more [] cur (:dao.stream/cursor outcome) n 1]\\\\\\\\n   112\\\\\\\\t    (if (or (>= n k) (nil? cur))\\\\\\\\n   113\\\\\\\\t      more\\\\\\\\n   114\\\\\\\\t      (let [r (middleware/apply-request h ctx\\\\\\\\n   115\\\\\\\\t                                        {:dao.stream.remote/op\\\\\\\\n   116\\\\\\\\t                                         :dao.stream/next\\\\\\\\n   117\\\\\\\\t                                         :dao.stream.remote/args [cur]})]\\\\\\\\n   118\\\\\\\\t        (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\\\\\\\\n   119\\\\\\\\t          (recur (conj more r) (:dao.stream/cursor r) (inc n))\\\\\\\\n   120\\\\\\\\t          (conj more r))))))\\\\\\\\n   121\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   122\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   123\\\\\\\\t(defn- write-answer!\\\\\\\\n   124\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Append one answer value toward the asker. A value the channel cannot\\\\\\\\n   125\\\\\\\\t   carry (invalid-value) is answered with the oversize protocol error\\\\\\\\n   126\\\\\\\\t   in place of that element, the read not skipped and no cursor\\\\\\\\n   127\\\\\\\\t   advanced past it; any other refused write leaves the request\\\\\\\\n   128\\\\\\\\t   unanswered.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   129\\\\\\\\t  [chan-writer answer]\\\\\\\\n   130\\\\\\\\t  (let [r (stream/append! chan-writer answer)]\\\\\\\\n   131\\\\\\\\t    (when (= :dao.stream/invalid-value (:dao.stream/outcome r))\\\\\\\\n   132\\\\\\\\t      (stream/append! chan-writer\\\\\\\\n   133\\\\\\\\t                      (error-answer (:dao.stream/identity answer)\\\\\\\\n   134\\\\\\\\t                                    (:dao.stream.remote/id answer)\\\\\\\\n   135\\\\\\\\t                                    :dao.stream.remote/oversize)))))\\\\\\\\n   136\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   137\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   138\\\\\\\\t(defn- answer!\\\\\\\\n   139\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The mirror's steps 1 to 4 for one well-formed request: look the\\\\\\\\n   140\\\\\\\\t   identity up in the table (not-found); check the op against the\\\\\\\\n   141\\\\\\\\t   entry's declared surface under the fixed mapping (no-surface);\\\\\\\\n   142\\\\\\\\t   answer descriptor from the entry's handle directly, adding the\\\\\\\\n   143\\\\\\\\t   declared surface, and apply cursor, next and append! through\\\\\\\\n   144\\\\\\\\t   apply-request with the channel context; append the answer with the\\\\\\\\n   145\\\\\\\\t   id and identity. For next with a budget k, and an ok first outcome,\\\\\\\\n   146\\\\\\\\t   the chase places the further outcomes under more.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   147\\\\\\\\t  [table chan-writer channel req]\\\\\\\\n   148\\\\\\\\t  (let [identity (:dao.stream/identity req)\\\\\\\\n   149\\\\\\\\t        id (:dao.stream.remote/id req)\\\\\\\\n   150\\\\\\\\t        op (:dao.stream.remote/op req)\\\\\\\\n   151\\\\\\\\t        entry (get table identity)]\\\\\\\\n   152\\\\\\\\t    (if-not entry\\\\\\\\n   153\\\\\\\\t      (write-answer! chan-writer\\\\\\\\n   154\\\\\\\\t                     (error-answer identity id\\\\\\\\n   155\\\\\\\\t                                   :dao.stream.remote/not-found))\\\\\\\\n   156\\\\\\\\t      (let [h (:handle entry)]\\\\\\\\n   157\\\\\\\\t        (if (= :dao.stream/descriptor op)\\\\\\\\n   158\\\\\\\\t          (write-answer!\\\\\\\\n   159\\\\\\\\t            chan-writer\\\\\\\\n   160\\\\\\\\t            (-> (stream/descriptor h)\\\\\\\\n   161\\\\\\\\t                (assoc :dao.stream.remote/id id\\\\\\\\n   162\\\\\\\\t                       :dao.stream/identity identity\\\\\\\\n   163\\\\\\\\t                       :dao.stream.remote/surface (:surface entry))))\\\\\\\\n   164\\\\\\\\t          (if-not (contains? (into #{} (:surface entry))\\\\\\\\n   165\\\\\\\\t                             (required-surface op))\\\\\\\\n   166\\\\\\\\t            (write-answer! chan-writer\\\\\\\\n   167\\\\\\\\t                           (error-answer identity id\\\\\\\\n   168\\\\\\\\t                                         :dao.stream.remote/no-surface))\\\\\\\\n   169\\\\\\\\t            (let [ctx {:dao.stream.remote/channel channel}\\\\\\\\n   170\\\\\\\\t                  outcome (middleware/apply-request h ctx req)\\\\\\\\n   171\\\\\\\\t                  more (when (and (= :dao.stream/next op)\\\\\\\\n   172\\\\\\\\t                                  (valid-budget? req)\\\\\\\\n   173\\\\\\\\t                                  (= :dao.stream/ok\\\\\\\\n   174\\\\\\\\t                                     (:dao.stream/outcome outcome)))\\\\\\\\n   175\\\\\\\\t                         (chase h ctx (:dao.stream.remote/budget req)\\\\\\\\n   176\\\\\\\\t                                outcome))\\\\\\\\n   177\\\\\\\\t                  answer (cond-> (assoc outcome\\\\\\\\n   178\\\\\\\\t                                        :dao.stream.remote/id id\\\\\\\\n   179\\\\\\\\t                                        :dao.stream/identity identity)\\\\\\\\n   180\\\\\\\\t                           (seq more) (assoc :dao.stream.remote/more\\\\\\\\n   181\\\\\\\\t                                             more))]\\\\\\\\n   182\\\\\\\\t              (write-answer! chan-writer answer))))))))\\\\\\\\n   183\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   184\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   185\\\\\\\\t(defn mirror-step\\\\\\\\n   186\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer requests against\\\\\\\\n   187\\\\\\\\t   its table: `(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) ->\\\\\\\\n   188\\\\\\\\t   cursor'`. For each request read from chan-reader at `cursor`, in\\\\\\\\n   189\\\\\\\\t   order, the four steps of 2.3 run; the mirror holds no state between\\\\\\\\n   190\\\\\\\\t   calls beyond the returned cursor. A value that is neither a\\\\\\\\n   191\\\\\\\\t   well-formed request nor a well-formed answer is dropped as malformed\\\\\\\\n   192\\\\\\\\t   wire input. The step stops when the channel reader answers blocked\\\\\\\\n   193\\\\\\\\t   or end; a gap adopts the reader's own recovery cursor, the lost wire\\\\\\\\n   194\\\\\\\\t   values being lost. The mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites\\\\\\\\n   195\\\\\\\\t   a cursor, an anchor or an outcome: the served stream is the\\\\\\\\n   196\\\\\\\\t   original, its cursors, its positions, its gap, its end.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   197\\\\\\\\t  [table chan-reader cursor chan-writer]\\\\\\\\n   198\\\\\\\\t  (let [channel (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor chan-reader))]\\\\\\\\n   199\\\\\\\\t    (loop [cursor cursor]\\\\\\\\n   200\\\\\\\\t      (let [r (stream/next chan-reader cursor)]\\\\\\\\n   201\\\\\\\\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\\\\\\\\n   202\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/ok\\\\\\\\n   203\\\\\\\\t          (do (when (well-formed-request? (:dao.stream/value r))\\\\\\\\n   204\\\\\\\\t                (answer! table chan-writer channel\\\\\\\\n   205\\\\\\\\t                         (:dao.stream/value r)))\\\\\\\\n   206\\\\\\\\t              (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r)))\\\\\\\\n   207\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/blocked cursor\\\\\\\\n   208\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/end cursor\\\\\\\\n   209\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/gap (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r))\\\\\\\\n   210\\\\\\\\t          cursor)))))\\\\\\\\n   211\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   212\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   213\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n   214\\\\\\\\t;; The link (2.4, 2.5)\\\\\\\\n   215\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n   216\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   217\\\\\\\\t(defn- new-link\\\\\\\\n   218\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"One link per channel descriptor, shared by every reflection through\\\\\\\\n   219\\\\\\\\t   that channel. A link holds the channel writer; the channel reader\\\\\\\\n   220\\\\\\\\t   and the link's own cursor on it; the outstanding request ids with\\\\\\\\n   221\\\\\\\\t   the request each was sent for; filed answers keyed by id; the more\\\\\\\\n   222\\\\\\\\t   outcomes installed at their served identity and the cursor that\\\\\\\\n   223\\\\\\\\t   precedes each; an optional event writer; and the policy data -- the\\\\\\\\n   224\\\\\\\\t   resend-after k and the budget k stamped on next requests, both\\\\\\\\n   225\\\\\\\\t   composition data.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   226\\\\\\\\t  [chan policy]\\\\\\\\n   227\\\\\\\\t  (atom {:reader (:reader chan)\\\\\\\\n   228\\\\\\\\t         :writer (:writer chan)\\\\\\\\n   229\\\\\\\\t         :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor\\\\\\\\n   230\\\\\\\\t                   (stream/cursor (:reader chan) :dao.stream/oldest))\\\\\\\\n   231\\\\\\\\t         :outstanding {}\\\\\\\\n   232\\\\\\\\t         :filed {}\\\\\\\\n   233\\\\\\\\t         :filed-cursors {}\\\\\\\\n   234\\\\\\\\t         :events (:dao.stream.remote/events policy)\\\\\\\\n   235\\\\\\\\t         :resend-after (:dao.stream.remote/resend-after policy)\\\\\\\\n   236\\\\\\\\t         :budget (:dao.stream.remote/budget policy)\\\\\\\\n   237\\\\\\\\t         :next-id 0\\\\\\\\n   238\\\\\\\\t         :channel-gone? false}))\\\\\\\\n   239\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   240\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   241\\\\\\\\t(defn- mint-id!\\\\\\\\n   242\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The next asker-minted id, unique per channel.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   243\\\\\\\\t  [link]\\\\\\\\n   244\\\\\\\\t  (let [n (:next-id @link)]\\\\\\\\n   245\\\\\\\\t    (swap! link assoc :next-id (inc n))\\\\\\\\n   246\\\\\\\\t    n))\\\\\\\\n   247\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   248\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   249\\\\\\\\t(defn- emit!\\\\\\\\n   250\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Append one event to the link's event writer when one is composed;\\\\\\\\n   251\\\\\\\\t   without an event writer the event is dropped.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   252\\\\\\\\t  [link v]\\\\\\\\n   253\\\\\\\\t  (when-some [w (:events @link)]\\\\\\\\n   254\\\\\\\\t    (stream/append! w v)))\\\\\\\\n   255\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   256\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   257\\\\\\\\t(defn- register!\\\\\\\\n   258\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Record `req` as outstanding under its id, with the request it was\\\\\\\\n   259\\\\\\\\t   sent for, its ask count, and the reflection that sent it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   260\\\\\\\\t  [link refl req]\\\\\\\\n   261\\\\\\\\t  (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id req)]\\\\\\\\n   262\\\\\\\\t    (swap! link assoc-in [:outstanding id]\\\\\\\\n   263\\\\\\\\t           {:req req :asks 0 :reflection refl})\\\\\\\\n   264\\\\\\\\t    (swap! refl update :ids conj id)))\\\\\\\\n   265\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   266\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   267\\\\\\\\t(defn- wire-request\\\\\\\\n   268\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"One request map for the channel, its id asker-minted; a next\\\\\\\\n   269\\\\\\\\t   request carries the link's budget when one is composed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   270\\\\\\\\t  [link refl op args]\\\\\\\\n   271\\\\\\\\t  (cond-> {:dao.stream/identity (:identity @refl)\\\\\\\\n   272\\\\\\\\t           :dao.stream.remote/op op\\\\\\\\n   273\\\\\\\\t           :dao.stream.remote/args args\\\\\\\\n   274\\\\\\\\t           :dao.stream.remote/id (mint-id! link)}\\\\\\\\n   275\\\\\\\\t    (and (= :dao.stream/next op) (some? (:budget @link)))\\\\\\\\n   276\\\\\\\\t    (assoc :dao.stream.remote/budget (:budget @link))))\\\\\\\\n   277\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   278\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   279\\\\\\\\t(defn- send-request!\\\\\\\\n   280\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Append one request map to the channel writer and register it as\\\\\\\\n   281\\\\\\\\t   outstanding only when the writer accepted the send: a send the\\\\\\\\n   282\\\\\\\\t   writer refuses with full leaves the request unsent and nothing\\\\\\\\n   283\\\\\\\\t   outstanding, so the operation answers as though unanswered and the\\\\\\\\n   284\\\\\\\\t   next ask sends again. Returns the writer's outcome.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   285\\\\\\\\t  [link refl req]\\\\\\\\n   286\\\\\\\\t  (let [r (stream/append! (:writer @link) req)]\\\\\\\\n   287\\\\\\\\t    (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\\\\\\\\n   288\\\\\\\\t      (register! link refl req))\\\\\\\\n   289\\\\\\\\t    r))\\\\\\\\n   290\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   291\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   292\\\\\\\\t(defn- count-ask!\\\\\\\\n   293\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Count one further ask of the outstanding request `id`; on reaching\\\\\\\\n   294\\\\\\\\t   the link's resend-after k, re-send it once -- the same id, a\\\\\\\\n   295\\\\\\\\t   duplicate of an idempotent operation, which recomputes the same or a\\\\\\\\n   296\\\\\\\\t   later, equally true answer -- and start counting afresh. append! is\\\\\\\\n   297\\\\\\\\t   never re-sent: it never stays outstanding beyond its send.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   298\\\\\\\\t  [link id]\\\\\\\\n   299\\\\\\\\t  (when-some [entry (get (:outstanding @link) id)]\\\\\\\\n   300\\\\\\\\t    (let [k (:resend-after @link)\\\\\\\\n   301\\\\\\\\t          asks (inc (:asks entry))]\\\\\\\\n   302\\\\\\\\t      (swap! link assoc-in [:outstanding id :asks] asks)\\\\\\\\n   303\\\\\\\\t      (when (and (integer? k) (pos? k) (<= k asks))\\\\\\\\n   304\\\\\\\\t        (stream/append! (:writer @link) (:req entry))\\\\\\\\n   305\\\\\\\\t        (swap! link assoc-in [:outstanding id :asks] 0)))))\\\\\\\\n   306\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   307\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   308\\\\\\\\t(defn- outstanding-for\\\\\\\\n   309\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The id of the link's outstanding request for `op` with `arg` sent by\\\\\\\\n   310\\\\\\\\t   `refl`, if any; the request each outstanding id was sent for makes\\\\\\\\n   311\\\\\\\\t   the correlation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   312\\\\\\\\t  [link refl op arg]\\\\\\\\n   313\\\\\\\\t  (when-some [[id _]\\\\\\\\n   314\\\\\\\\t              (first\\\\\\\\n   315\\\\\\\\t                (filter (fn [[_ e]]\\\\\\\\n   316\\\\\\\\t                          (let [r (:req e)]\\\\\\\\n   317\\\\\\\\t                            (and (= refl (:reflection e))\\\\\\\\n   318\\\\\\\\t                                 (= op (:dao.stream.remote/op r))\\\\\\\\n   319\\\\\\\\t                                 (= arg (first\\\\\\\\n   320\\\\\\\\t                                          (:dao.stream.remote/args r))))))\\\\\\\\n   321\\\\\\\\t                        (:outstanding @link)))]\\\\\\\\n   322\\\\\\\\t    id))\\\\\\\\n   323\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   324\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   325\\\\\\\\t(defn- filed-for\\\\\\\\n   326\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The first filed answer for `op` with `arg` sent by `refl`, in id\\\\\\\\n   327\\\\\\\\t   order, as [id entry]. A filed answer keeps the request it was sent\\\\\\\\n   328\\\\\\\\t   for and the reflection that sent it -- an answer belongs to the\\\\\\\\n   329\\\\\\\\t   operation that caused it -- so it is found whether or not its\\\\\\\\n   330\\\\\\\\t   outstanding id was abandoned.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   331\\\\\\\\t  [link refl op arg]\\\\\\\\n   332\\\\\\\\t  (let [filed (:filed @link)]\\\\\\\\n   333\\\\\\\\t    (when-some [id\\\\\\\\n   334\\\\\\\\t                (first\\\\\\\\n   335\\\\\\\\t                  (filter (fn [i]\\\\\\\\n   336\\\\\\\\t                            (let [e (get filed i)\\\\\\\\n   337\\\\\\\\t                                  r (:req e)]\\\\\\\\n   338\\\\\\\\t                              (and (= refl (:reflection e))\\\\\\\\n   339\\\\\\\\t                                   (= op (:dao.stream.remote/op r))\\\\\\\\n   340\\\\\\\\t                                   (= arg (first\\\\\\\\n   341\\\\\\\\t                                            (:dao.stream.remote/args r))))))\\\\\\\\n   342\\\\\\\\t                          (sort (keys filed))))]\\\\\\\\n   343\\\\\\\\t      [id (get filed id)])))\\\\\\\\n   344\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   345\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   346\\\\\\\\t(defn- bare-outcome\\\\\\\\n   347\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The source's own outcome map, verbatim: the correlation keys the\\\\\\\\n   348\\\\\\\\t   answer envelope added are removed, nothing else.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   349\\\\\\\\t  [ans]\\\\\\\\n   350\\\\\\\\t  (dissoc ans :dao.stream.remote/id :dao.stream/identity))\\\\\\\\n   351\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   352\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   353\\\\\\\\t(defn- translated\\\\\\\\n   354\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"A filed protocol error at the reflection: transport-error with the\\\\\\\\n   355\\\\\\\\t   error's name as the reason. not-found marks the reflection gone at\\\\\\\\n   356\\\\\\\\t   filing; no-surface and oversize mark nothing.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   357\\\\\\\\t  [e]\\\\\\\\n   358\\\\\\\\t  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\\\\\\\\n   359\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream.remote/reason e})\\\\\\\\n   360\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   361\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   362\\\\\\\\t(defn- learn!\\\\\\\\n   363\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The attach probe's ok: record the source's descriptor and declared\\\\\\\\n   364\\\\\\\\t   surface, plus :closable.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   365\\\\\\\\t  [refl answer]\\\\\\\\n   366\\\\\\\\t  (swap! refl assoc\\\\\\\\n   367\\\\\\\\t         :source-descriptor (:dao.stream/descriptor answer)\\\\\\\\n   368\\\\\\\\t         :surface (conj (into #{} (:dao.stream.remote/surface answer))\\\\\\\\n   369\\\\\\\\t                        :closable)))\\\\\\\\n   370\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   371\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   372\\\\\\\\t(defn- install-more!\\\\\\\\n   373\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Install each more outcome under the answer's served identity and at\\\\\\\\n   374\\\\\\\\t   the cursor that precedes it, so a later next at that cursor by a\\\\\\\\n   375\\\\\\\\t   reflection of the same stream returns the source's own outcome\\\\\\\\n   376\\\\\\\\t   without a request. The identity is half of the key: two served\\\\\\\\n   377\\\\\\\\t   identities can carry equal cursor values, and an outcome belongs to\\\\\\\\n   378\\\\\\\\t   its own stream.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   379\\\\\\\\t  [link answer]\\\\\\\\n   380\\\\\\\\t  (let [identity (:dao.stream/identity answer)]\\\\\\\\n   381\\\\\\\\t    (loop [cur (:dao.stream/cursor answer)\\\\\\\\n   382\\\\\\\\t           more (:dao.stream.remote/more answer)]\\\\\\\\n   383\\\\\\\\t      (when (and cur (seq more))\\\\\\\\n   384\\\\\\\\t        (let [[o & further] more]\\\\\\\\n   385\\\\\\\\t          (swap! link assoc-in [:filed-cursors [identity cur]] o)\\\\\\\\n   386\\\\\\\\t          (recur (:dao.stream/cursor o) further))))))\\\\\\\\n   387\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   388\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   389\\\\\\\\t(defn- absorb!\\\\\\\\n   390\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Consume one value read off the channel. A well-formed answer whose\\\\\\\\n   391\\\\\\\\t   id is outstanding is taken: a not-found error marks the reflection\\\\\\\\n   392\\\\\\\\t   gone; the attach probe's ok records the source's descriptor and\\\\\\\\n   393\\\\\\\\t   declared surface; an append!'s source outcome is emitted on the\\\\\\\\n   394\\\\\\\\t   event writer, correlated by id; any other answer is filed under its\\\\\\\\n   395\\\\\\\\t   id and its more outcomes installed. Answers whose id is not\\\\\\\\n   396\\\\\\\\t   outstanding, and values that are not well-formed answers, are\\\\\\\\n   397\\\\\\\\t   dropped as diagnostics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   398\\\\\\\\t  [link v]\\\\\\\\n   399\\\\\\\\t  (when (well-formed-answer? v)\\\\\\\\n   400\\\\\\\\t    (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id v)]\\\\\\\\n   401\\\\\\\\t      (when-some [entry (get (:outstanding @link) id)]\\\\\\\\n   402\\\\\\\\t        (let [refl (:reflection entry)\\\\\\\\n   403\\\\\\\\t              req (:req entry)\\\\\\\\n   404\\\\\\\\t              op (:dao.stream.remote/op req)\\\\\\\\n   405\\\\\\\\t              e (:dao.stream.remote/error v)]\\\\\\\\n   406\\\\\\\\t          (swap! link update :outstanding dissoc id)\\\\\\\\n   407\\\\\\\\t          (swap! refl update :ids disj id)\\\\\\\\n   408\\\\\\\\t          (cond\\\\\\\\n   409\\\\\\\\t            (= :dao.stream.remote/not-found e)\\\\\\\\n   410\\\\\\\\t            (do (swap! refl assoc :gone? true)\\\\\\\\n   411\\\\\\\\t                (when (or (= :dao.stream/descriptor op)\\\\\\\\n   412\\\\\\\\t                          (= :dao.stream/append! op))\\\\\\\\n   413\\\\\\\\t                  (emit! link v)))\\\\\\\\n   414\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   415\\\\\\\\t            (= :dao.stream/descriptor op)\\\\\\\\n   416\\\\\\\\t            ;; The attach probe's confirmation. ok records; a\\\\\\\\n   417\\\\\\\\t            ;; no-surface or oversize error marks nothing.\\\\\\\\n   418\\\\\\\\t            (when (nil? e)\\\\\\\\n   419\\\\\\\\t              (learn! refl v)\\\\\\\\n   420\\\\\\\\t              (emit! link v))\\\\\\\\n   421\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   422\\\\\\\\t            (= :dao.stream/append! op)\\\\\\\\n   423\\\\\\\\t            (emit! link v)\\\\\\\\n   424\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   425\\\\\\\\t            :else\\\\\\\\n   426\\\\\\\\t            (do (swap! link assoc-in\\\\\\\\n   427\\\\\\\\t                       [:filed id] {:req req :ans v :reflection refl})\\\\\\\\n   428\\\\\\\\t                (install-more! link v))))))))\\\\\\\\n   429\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   430\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   431\\\\\\\\t(defn- channel-loss!\\\\\\\\n   432\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The channel reader answered end: the channel is gone. The link\\\\\\\\n   433\\\\\\\\t   abandons its outstanding ids, reporting each abandoned append! on\\\\\\\\n   434\\\\\\\\t   the event writer as append-unknown; filed answers are kept and\\\\\\\\n   435\\\\\\\\t   still returned; reattachment is the caller's policy.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   436\\\\\\\\t  [link]\\\\\\\\n   437\\\\\\\\t  (doseq [[id entry] (:outstanding @link)]\\\\\\\\n   438\\\\\\\\t    (when (= :dao.stream/append! (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req entry)))\\\\\\\\n   439\\\\\\\\t      (emit! link {:dao.stream.remote/event\\\\\\\\n   440\\\\\\\\t                   :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\\\\\\\\n   441\\\\\\\\t                   :dao.stream.remote/id id}))\\\\\\\\n   442\\\\\\\\t    (swap! (:reflection entry) update :ids disj id))\\\\\\\\n   443\\\\\\\\t  (swap! link assoc :outstanding {} :channel-gone? true))\\\\\\\\n   444\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   445\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   446\\\\\\\\t(defn- drain!\\\\\\\\n   447\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Read the link's channel reader to blocked, filing each answer: the\\\\\\\\n   448\\\\\\\\t   caller's own polling is the cadence and no driver step exists. An\\\\\\\\n   449\\\\\\\\t   end is the channel's loss; a gap adopts the reader's own recovery\\\\\\\\n   450\\\\\\\\t   cursor, the lost wire values being lost. Each drain is also one\\\\\\\\n   451\\\\\\\\t   further ask of an outstanding descriptor probe -- the probe's\\\\\\\\n   452\\\\\\\\t   caller never asks it remotely -- so resend-after re-sends it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   453\\\\\\\\t  [link]\\\\\\\\n   454\\\\\\\\t  (when-not (:channel-gone? @link)\\\\\\\\n   455\\\\\\\\t    (doseq [[_ e] (:outstanding @link)]\\\\\\\\n   456\\\\\\\\t      (when (= :dao.stream/descriptor (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req e)))\\\\\\\\n   457\\\\\\\\t        (count-ask! link (:dao.stream.remote/id (:req e)))))\\\\\\\\n   458\\\\\\\\t    (loop []\\\\\\\\n   459\\\\\\\\t      (let [r (stream/next (:reader @link) (:cursor @link))]\\\\\\\\n   460\\\\\\\\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\\\\\\\\n   461\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/ok\\\\\\\\n   462\\\\\\\\t          (do (absorb! link (:dao.stream/value r))\\\\\\\\n   463\\\\\\\\t              (swap! link assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\\\\\\\\n   464\\\\\\\\t              (recur))\\\\\\\\n   465\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/blocked nil\\\\\\\\n   466\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/end (channel-loss! link)\\\\\\\\n   467\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/gap\\\\\\\\n   468\\\\\\\\t          (do (swap! link assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r)) nil)\\\\\\\\n   469\\\\\\\\t          nil)))))\\\\\\\\n   470\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   471\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   472\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n   473\\\\\\\\t;; The reflection (2.4)\\\\\\\\n   474\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n   475\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   476\\\\\\\\t(defn- result\\\\\\\\n   477\\\\\\\\t  [outcome]\\\\\\\\n   478\\\\\\\\t  {:dao.stream/outcome outcome})\\\\\\\\n   479\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   480\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   481\\\\\\\\t(defn- refl-descriptor\\\\\\\\n   482\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Local: ok with the remote descriptor the reflection was attached\\\\\\\\n   483\\\\\\\\t   with and the identity -- the same identity as the source's own\\\\\\\\n   484\\\\\\\\t   descriptor, different reachability. A name outlives what it named:\\\\\\\\n   485\\\\\\\\t   descriptor still answers ok once gone, once closed, and after\\\\\\\\n   486\\\\\\\\t   channel loss.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   487\\\\\\\\t  [refl]\\\\\\\\n   488\\\\\\\\t  (drain! (:link @refl))\\\\\\\\n   489\\\\\\\\t  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\\\\\\\\n   490\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream/descriptor (:descriptor @refl)\\\\\\\\n   491\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream/identity (:identity @refl)})\\\\\\\\n   492\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   493\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   494\\\\\\\\t(defn- surface-lacks?\\\\\\\\n   495\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"True when the source's declared surface is learned and lacks the\\\\\\\\n   496\\\\\\\\t   surface `op` needs. Before the probe's answer the reflection\\\\\\\\n   497\\\\\\\\t   declares #{:reader :writer :closable}, so every operation is\\\\\\\\n   498\\\\\\\\t   attempted; an operation the source turns out to lack is answered as\\\\\\\\n   499\\\\\\\\t   the no-surface case.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   500\\\\\\\\t  [refl op]\\\\\\\\n   501\\\\\\\\t  (let [s (:surface @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   502\\\\\\\\t    (and (some? s) (not (contains? s (required-surface op))))))\\\\\\\\n   503\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   504\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   505\\\\\\\\t(defn- filed-answer-outcome\\\\\\\\n   506\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The outcome a filed answer yields: a protocol error translated per\\\\\\\\n   507\\\\\\\\t   the rules, otherwise the source's outcome map verbatim.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   508\\\\\\\\t  [ans]\\\\\\\\n   509\\\\\\\\t  (or (some-> (:dao.stream.remote/error ans) translated)\\\\\\\\n   510\\\\\\\\t      (bare-outcome ans)))\\\\\\\\n   511\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   512\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   513\\\\\\\\t(defn- filed-cursor\\\\\\\\n   514\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"A filed answer for anchor `a` sent by `refl`, returned without\\\\\\\\n   515\\\\\\\\t   being forgotten: minting is idempotent, so the answer stays as true\\\\\\\\n   516\\\\\\\\t   as when it was minted.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   517\\\\\\\\t  [link refl a]\\\\\\\\n   518\\\\\\\\t  (when-some [[_ entry] (filed-for link refl :dao.stream/cursor a)]\\\\\\\\n   519\\\\\\\\t    (filed-answer-outcome (:ans entry))))\\\\\\\\n   520\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   521\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   522\\\\\\\\t(defn- filed-next!\\\\\\\\n   523\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"A filed answer for cursor `c` sent by `refl`: the outcome an\\\\\\\\n   524\\\\\\\\t   installed more element put at `c` for the stream `refl` serves,\\\\\\\\n   525\\\\\\\\t   else the filed answer of a next request for `c`; returned and\\\\\\\\n   526\\\\\\\\t   forgotten.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   527\\\\\\\\t  [link refl c]\\\\\\\\n   528\\\\\\\\t  (let [k [(:identity @refl) c]]\\\\\\\\n   529\\\\\\\\t    (or (when-some [o (get (:filed-cursors @link) k)]\\\\\\\\n   530\\\\\\\\t          (swap! link update :filed-cursors dissoc k)\\\\\\\\n   531\\\\\\\\t          (filed-answer-outcome o))\\\\\\\\n   532\\\\\\\\t        (when-some [[id entry]\\\\\\\\n   533\\\\\\\\t                    (filed-for link refl :dao.stream/next c)]\\\\\\\\n   534\\\\\\\\t          (swap! link update :filed dissoc id)\\\\\\\\n   535\\\\\\\\t          (filed-answer-outcome (:ans entry))))))\\\\\\\\n   536\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   537\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   538\\\\\\\\t(defn- retry-read\\\\\\\\n   539\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The answer a just-sent cursor request gives: the source's map,\\\\\\\\n   540\\\\\\\\t   invalid-anchor included, arrives later as an answer.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   541\\\\\\\\t  []\\\\\\\\n   542\\\\\\\\t  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\\\\\\\\n   543\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream/retry? true})\\\\\\\\n   544\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   545\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   546\\\\\\\\t(defn- send-or-count!\\\\\\\\n   547\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Send the request for `op` with `arg` when none of this reflection's\\\\\\\\n   548\\\\\\\\t   own is outstanding; otherwise count one further ask of the\\\\\\\\n   549\\\\\\\\t   outstanding request, which re-sends it at the link's resend-after\\\\\\\\n   550\\\\\\\\t   k.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   551\\\\\\\\t  [link refl op arg]\\\\\\\\n   552\\\\\\\\t  (if-some [id (outstanding-for link refl op arg)]\\\\\\\\n   553\\\\\\\\t    (count-ask! link id)\\\\\\\\n   554\\\\\\\\t    (send-request! link refl (wire-request link refl op [arg]))))\\\\\\\\n   555\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   556\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   557\\\\\\\\t(defn- refl-cursor\\\\\\\\n   558\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The cursor operation: a filed answer for the anchor is returned,\\\\\\\\n   559\\\\\\\\t   and a filed protocol error translated; otherwise a cursor request\\\\\\\\n   560\\\\\\\\t   is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is transport-error\\\\\\\\n   561\\\\\\\\t   with :dao.stream/retry? true. Once closed, cursor answers closed;\\\\\\\\n   562\\\\\\\\t   once gone, transport-error naming not-found.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   563\\\\\\\\t  [refl a]\\\\\\\\n   564\\\\\\\\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   565\\\\\\\\t    (drain! link)\\\\\\\\n   566\\\\\\\\t    (cond\\\\\\\\n   567\\\\\\\\t      (:closed? @refl) (result :dao.stream/closed)\\\\\\\\n   568\\\\\\\\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\\\\\\\\n   569\\\\\\\\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/cursor)\\\\\\\\n   570\\\\\\\\t      (translated :dao.stream.remote/no-surface)\\\\\\\\n   571\\\\\\\\t      :else\\\\\\\\n   572\\\\\\\\t      (or (filed-cursor link refl a)\\\\\\\\n   573\\\\\\\\t          (when-not (:channel-gone? @link)\\\\\\\\n   574\\\\\\\\t            (send-or-count! link refl :dao.stream/cursor a)\\\\\\\\n   575\\\\\\\\t            (retry-read))\\\\\\\\n   576\\\\\\\\t          (translated :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone)))))\\\\\\\\n   577\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   578\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   579\\\\\\\\t(defn- refl-next\\\\\\\\n   580\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The next operation: a filed answer for the cursor is returned and\\\\\\\\n   581\\\\\\\\t   forgotten; otherwise a next request is sent if none is outstanding,\\\\\\\\n   582\\\\\\\\t   and the answer is blocked -- nothing is observable at this position\\\\\\\\n   583\\\\\\\\t   through this handle yet. After close, filed outcomes then end. The\\\\\\\\n   584\\\\\\\\t   reflection never judges a cursor: cursor-mismatch and\\\\\\\\n   585\\\\\\\\t   invalid-cursor are only ever relayed from the source.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   586\\\\\\\\t  [refl c]\\\\\\\\n   587\\\\\\\\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   588\\\\\\\\t    (drain! link)\\\\\\\\n   589\\\\\\\\t    (cond\\\\\\\\n   590\\\\\\\\t      (:closed? @refl)\\\\\\\\n   591\\\\\\\\t      (or (filed-next! link refl c) (result :dao.stream/end))\\\\\\\\n   592\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   593\\\\\\\\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\\\\\\\\n   594\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   595\\\\\\\\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/next)\\\\\\\\n   596\\\\\\\\t      (translated :dao.stream.remote/no-surface)\\\\\\\\n   597\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   598\\\\\\\\t      :else\\\\\\\\n   599\\\\\\\\t      (or (filed-next! link refl c)\\\\\\\\n   600\\\\\\\\t          (when-not (:channel-gone? @link)\\\\\\\\n   601\\\\\\\\t            (send-or-count! link refl :dao.stream/next c)\\\\\\\\n   602\\\\\\\\t            (result :dao.stream/blocked))\\\\\\\\n   603\\\\\\\\t          (translated :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone)))))\\\\\\\\n   604\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   605\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   606\\\\\\\\t(defn- refl-append\\\\\\\\n   607\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The append! operation: send the request and return the channel\\\\\\\\n   608\\\\\\\\t   writer's outcome -- ok is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly\\\\\\\\n   609\\\\\\\\t   the contract's Writing rule for a transport that carries values\\\\\\\\n   610\\\\\\\\t   toward a stream elsewhere. The source's outcome is emitted on the\\\\\\\\n   611\\\\\\\\t   event writer when it is filed, correlated by id; append! never\\\\\\\\n   612\\\\\\\\t   answers the source's full. Only an accepted send is in flight: a\\\\\\\\n   613\\\\\\\\t   refused append! never crossed, so its effect is not unknown.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   614\\\\\\\\t  [refl v]\\\\\\\\n   615\\\\\\\\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   616\\\\\\\\t    (drain! link)\\\\\\\\n   617\\\\\\\\t    (cond\\\\\\\\n   618\\\\\\\\t      (:closed? @refl) (result :dao.stream/closed)\\\\\\\\n   619\\\\\\\\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\\\\\\\\n   620\\\\\\\\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/append!)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n./collab/1790479176072-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-confirm.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:16:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_7\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790476861675-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-gate.gpt-6-sol.findings.md collab/1790476870000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2-fixes.glm-flash.report.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-27 02:41:46 GMT\\\\\\\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-27 09:41:46 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nP1 | [remote.cljc:278](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:278) | `send-request!` registers a `descriptor`, `cursor`, or `next` request even when the channel writer refuses it with `full`. Later calls find that unsent request outstanding ([line 543](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:543)); with no `resend-after`, they never send it. A transient full channel can therefore strand the attach probe or a read indefinitely. | Register only accepted sends, or retry a refused send on the next ask.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nP1 | [remote.cljc:374](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:374) | Budget chase outcomes are keyed only by cursor and consumed by any reflection on the shared link ([line 520](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:520)). Two served identities can use equal cursor values, letting a `next` on one reflection return the other stream’s outcome. | Key installed outcomes by served identity and cursor, while retaining sharing among reflections of the same stream.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOf the ten reported readings, channel context, budget minting, whole-answer oversize, reader gap recovery, probe resend cadence, append event handling, stated precedence, correlation-key stripping, and protocol implementation are acceptable readings on inspection. Filing by reflection and operation is acceptable for direct answers; the shared `:more` filing has the cross-identity defect above. The mirror’s dispatch order and middleware path match §2.3, and I found no privileged server or client role. The orchestrator’s JVM, Node, and Dart results are accepted as supplied; I did not rerun suites.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\\\\\\\\nSign-off: DENIEDBoth P1 findings fixed; only the two permitted files touched; gate-confirmed behavior preserved (nine ratified readings, mirror dispatch order, no privilege roles — all pre-existing tests unchanged and green).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Fix 1 — refused send no longer registered (P1, remote.cljc:278)**\\\\\\\\n`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:278-289`: `send-request!` now calls `register!` only when the writer answered `:dao.stream/ok`. A send refused with `full` leaves the request unsent and nothing outstanding, so `outstanding-for` (remote.cljc:549) does not strand the next ask — the retry cases re-send on the next ask, exactly per 2.4's \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"leaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\". This covers the attach probe (remote.cljc:823) and cursor/next sends; `refl-append` (remote.cljc:620-628) already registered only accepted appends and is untouched.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Fix 2 — installed outcomes keyed by served identity AND cursor (P1, remote.cljc:374)**\\\\\\\\n- `remote.cljc:369-387` `install-more!`: installs each more outcome under `[:filed-cursors [identity cur]]`, with `identity` taken from the answer envelope — reflections of the same stream share the same key, so sharing on the link is retained.\\\\\\\\n- `remote.cljc:524-534` `filed-next!`: consumes the installed outcome at `[(:identity @refl) c]`; a next on one identity can no longer return another stream's outcome at an equal cursor.\\\\\\\\n- `remote.cljc:217-226`: `new-link` docstring updated to state the identity+cursor keying. No other consumer of `:filed-cursors` exists.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Tests added** (`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc`)\\\\\\\\n- Helper `full-then-forward-writer` (:148): a writer refusing the first n appends with `:dao.stream/full`, then forwarding.\\\\\\\\n- Helper `list-stream` (:163): a minimal served reader whose cursors are bare positions, so two served identities can hold equal cursor values (ringbuffer cursors embed a random-uuid identity and can never be `=`).\\\\\\\\n- `a-full-refused-send-leaves-nothing-outstanding` (:583): attach and first read pass through a refusing writer; the read answers unanswered-style (`transport-error` + `retry?`), nothing crosses; when the writer recovers the very next ask sends (1 cursor request on the wire — 0 under the old code, which strands the outstanding entry), and after one `serve!` the following ask returns the source's own cursor outcome.\\\\\\\\n- `installed-outcomes-stay-with-their-own-stream` (:488): two identities \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"p\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"q\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" on one link, equal cursor values asserted; each reflection's next returns only its own stream's outcomes (`:a1/:a2` vs `:b1/:b2`); a third reflection of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"p\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" shares p's installed outcome without a request, and q's installed outcome is untouched.\\\\\\\\n- ns docstring extended with the two new proofs.\\\\\\\\n- Bite check: with both fixes temporarily reverted, exactly these two tests fail (6 assertions); all 18 pre-existing tests pass. Fixed file restored from /tmp; no git state operations used.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Lane verification (sequential, solo, exact counts)**\\\\\\\\n- JVM `mise exec -- clojure -M:test`: Ran 2,254 tests containing 183,208 assertions. 0 failures, 0 errors. (baseline 2,252/183,193/0; +2/+15)\\\\\\\\n- Node `mise exec -- bb test:cljs`: Ran 2,166 tests containing 49,814 assertions. 0 failures, 0 errors. (baseline 2,164/49,799/0; +2/+15)\\\\\\\\n- Dart `mise exec -- bb test:cljd`: All tests passed! — 2,128 passed. (baseline 2,126; +2; new tests verified present in the fresh `test/cljd-out/dao/stream/remote-test_test.dart`)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nHygiene: `cljstyle check` clean on both files; `clj-kondo` 0 errors, 0 warnings; pure ASCII; no line over 80 columns; no leftover diagnostics; no tracked file modified and no commit/stage/checkout/reset/stash performed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStatus: COMPLETE\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n./collab/1790479176072-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-confirm.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:18:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_8\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc | sed -n '389,855p'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"   389\\\\\\\\t(defn- absorb!\\\\\\\\n   390\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Consume one value read off the channel. A well-formed answer whose\\\\\\\\n   391\\\\\\\\t   id is outstanding is taken: a not-found error marks the reflection\\\\\\\\n   392\\\\\\\\t   gone; the attach probe's ok records the source's descriptor and\\\\\\\\n   393\\\\\\\\t   declared surface; an append!'s source outcome is emitted on the\\\\\\\\n   394\\\\\\\\t   event writer, correlated by id; any other answer is filed under its\\\\\\\\n   395\\\\\\\\t   id and its more outcomes installed. Answers whose id is not\\\\\\\\n   396\\\\\\\\t   outstanding, and values that are not well-formed answers, are\\\\\\\\n   397\\\\\\\\t   dropped as diagnostics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   398\\\\\\\\t  [link v]\\\\\\\\n   399\\\\\\\\t  (when (well-formed-answer? v)\\\\\\\\n   400\\\\\\\\t    (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id v)]\\\\\\\\n   401\\\\\\\\t      (when-some [entry (get (:outstanding @link) id)]\\\\\\\\n   402\\\\\\\\t        (let [refl (:reflection entry)\\\\\\\\n   403\\\\\\\\t              req (:req entry)\\\\\\\\n   404\\\\\\\\t              op (:dao.stream.remote/op req)\\\\\\\\n   405\\\\\\\\t              e (:dao.stream.remote/error v)]\\\\\\\\n   406\\\\\\\\t          (swap! link update :outstanding dissoc id)\\\\\\\\n   407\\\\\\\\t          (swap! refl update :ids disj id)\\\\\\\\n   408\\\\\\\\t          (cond\\\\\\\\n   409\\\\\\\\t            (= :dao.stream.remote/not-found e)\\\\\\\\n   410\\\\\\\\t            (do (swap! refl assoc :gone? true)\\\\\\\\n   411\\\\\\\\t                (when (or (= :dao.stream/descriptor op)\\\\\\\\n   412\\\\\\\\t                          (= :dao.stream/append! op))\\\\\\\\n   413\\\\\\\\t                  (emit! link v)))\\\\\\\\n   414\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   415\\\\\\\\t            (= :dao.stream/descriptor op)\\\\\\\\n   416\\\\\\\\t            ;; The attach probe's confirmation. ok records; a\\\\\\\\n   417\\\\\\\\t            ;; no-surface or oversize error marks nothing.\\\\\\\\n   418\\\\\\\\t            (when (nil? e)\\\\\\\\n   419\\\\\\\\t              (learn! refl v)\\\\\\\\n   420\\\\\\\\t              (emit! link v))\\\\\\\\n   421\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   422\\\\\\\\t            (= :dao.stream/append! op)\\\\\\\\n   423\\\\\\\\t            (emit! link v)\\\\\\\\n   424\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   425\\\\\\\\t            :else\\\\\\\\n   426\\\\\\\\t            (do (swap! link assoc-in\\\\\\\\n   427\\\\\\\\t                       [:filed id] {:req req :ans v :reflection refl})\\\\\\\\n   428\\\\\\\\t                (install-more! link v))))))))\\\\\\\\n   429\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   430\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   431\\\\\\\\t(defn- channel-loss!\\\\\\\\n   432\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The channel reader answered end: the channel is gone. The link\\\\\\\\n   433\\\\\\\\t   abandons its outstanding ids, reporting each abandoned append! on\\\\\\\\n   434\\\\\\\\t   the event writer as append-unknown; filed answers are kept and\\\\\\\\n   435\\\\\\\\t   still returned; reattachment is the caller's policy.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   436\\\\\\\\t  [link]\\\\\\\\n   437\\\\\\\\t  (doseq [[id entry] (:outstanding @link)]\\\\\\\\n   438\\\\\\\\t    (when (= :dao.stream/append! (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req entry)))\\\\\\\\n   439\\\\\\\\t      (emit! link {:dao.stream.remote/event\\\\\\\\n   440\\\\\\\\t                   :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\\\\\\\\n   441\\\\\\\\t                   :dao.stream.remote/id id}))\\\\\\\\n   442\\\\\\\\t    (swap! (:reflection entry) update :ids disj id))\\\\\\\\n   443\\\\\\\\t  (swap! link assoc :outstanding {} :channel-gone? true))\\\\\\\\n   444\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   445\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   446\\\\\\\\t(defn- drain!\\\\\\\\n   447\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Read the link's channel reader to blocked, filing each answer: the\\\\\\\\n   448\\\\\\\\t   caller's own polling is the cadence and no driver step exists. An\\\\\\\\n   449\\\\\\\\t   end is the channel's loss; a gap adopts the reader's own recovery\\\\\\\\n   450\\\\\\\\t   cursor, the lost wire values being lost. Each drain is also one\\\\\\\\n   451\\\\\\\\t   further ask of an outstanding descriptor probe -- the probe's\\\\\\\\n   452\\\\\\\\t   caller never asks it remotely -- so resend-after re-sends it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   453\\\\\\\\t  [link]\\\\\\\\n   454\\\\\\\\t  (when-not (:channel-gone? @link)\\\\\\\\n   455\\\\\\\\t    (doseq [[_ e] (:outstanding @link)]\\\\\\\\n   456\\\\\\\\t      (when (= :dao.stream/descriptor (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req e)))\\\\\\\\n   457\\\\\\\\t        (count-ask! link (:dao.stream.remote/id (:req e)))))\\\\\\\\n   458\\\\\\\\t    (loop []\\\\\\\\n   459\\\\\\\\t      (let [r (stream/next (:reader @link) (:cursor @link))]\\\\\\\\n   460\\\\\\\\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\\\\\\\\n   461\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/ok\\\\\\\\n   462\\\\\\\\t          (do (absorb! link (:dao.stream/value r))\\\\\\\\n   463\\\\\\\\t              (swap! link assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\\\\\\\\n   464\\\\\\\\t              (recur))\\\\\\\\n   465\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/blocked nil\\\\\\\\n   466\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/end (channel-loss! link)\\\\\\\\n   467\\\\\\\\t          :dao.stream/gap\\\\\\\\n   468\\\\\\\\t          (do (swap! link assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r)) nil)\\\\\\\\n   469\\\\\\\\t          nil)))))\\\\\\\\n   470\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   471\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   472\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n   473\\\\\\\\t;; The reflection (2.4)\\\\\\\\n   474\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n   475\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   476\\\\\\\\t(defn- result\\\\\\\\n   477\\\\\\\\t  [outcome]\\\\\\\\n   478\\\\\\\\t  {:dao.stream/outcome outcome})\\\\\\\\n   479\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   480\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   481\\\\\\\\t(defn- refl-descriptor\\\\\\\\n   482\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Local: ok with the remote descriptor the reflection was attached\\\\\\\\n   483\\\\\\\\t   with and the identity -- the same identity as the source's own\\\\\\\\n   484\\\\\\\\t   descriptor, different reachability. A name outlives what it named:\\\\\\\\n   485\\\\\\\\t   descriptor still answers ok once gone, once closed, and after\\\\\\\\n   486\\\\\\\\t   channel loss.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   487\\\\\\\\t  [refl]\\\\\\\\n   488\\\\\\\\t  (drain! (:link @refl))\\\\\\\\n   489\\\\\\\\t  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\\\\\\\\n   490\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream/descriptor (:descriptor @refl)\\\\\\\\n   491\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream/identity (:identity @refl)})\\\\\\\\n   492\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   493\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   494\\\\\\\\t(defn- surface-lacks?\\\\\\\\n   495\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"True when the source's declared surface is learned and lacks the\\\\\\\\n   496\\\\\\\\t   surface `op` needs. Before the probe's answer the reflection\\\\\\\\n   497\\\\\\\\t   declares #{:reader :writer :closable}, so every operation is\\\\\\\\n   498\\\\\\\\t   attempted; an operation the source turns out to lack is answered as\\\\\\\\n   499\\\\\\\\t   the no-surface case.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   500\\\\\\\\t  [refl op]\\\\\\\\n   501\\\\\\\\t  (let [s (:surface @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   502\\\\\\\\t    (and (some? s) (not (contains? s (required-surface op))))))\\\\\\\\n   503\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   504\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   505\\\\\\\\t(defn- filed-answer-outcome\\\\\\\\n   506\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The outcome a filed answer yields: a protocol error translated per\\\\\\\\n   507\\\\\\\\t   the rules, otherwise the source's outcome map verbatim.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   508\\\\\\\\t  [ans]\\\\\\\\n   509\\\\\\\\t  (or (some-> (:dao.stream.remote/error ans) translated)\\\\\\\\n   510\\\\\\\\t      (bare-outcome ans)))\\\\\\\\n   511\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   512\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   513\\\\\\\\t(defn- filed-cursor\\\\\\\\n   514\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"A filed answer for anchor `a` sent by `refl`, returned without\\\\\\\\n   515\\\\\\\\t   being forgotten: minting is idempotent, so the answer stays as true\\\\\\\\n   516\\\\\\\\t   as when it was minted.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   517\\\\\\\\t  [link refl a]\\\\\\\\n   518\\\\\\\\t  (when-some [[_ entry] (filed-for link refl :dao.stream/cursor a)]\\\\\\\\n   519\\\\\\\\t    (filed-answer-outcome (:ans entry))))\\\\\\\\n   520\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   521\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   522\\\\\\\\t(defn- filed-next!\\\\\\\\n   523\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"A filed answer for cursor `c` sent by `refl`: the outcome an\\\\\\\\n   524\\\\\\\\t   installed more element put at `c` for the stream `refl` serves,\\\\\\\\n   525\\\\\\\\t   else the filed answer of a next request for `c`; returned and\\\\\\\\n   526\\\\\\\\t   forgotten.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   527\\\\\\\\t  [link refl c]\\\\\\\\n   528\\\\\\\\t  (let [k [(:identity @refl) c]]\\\\\\\\n   529\\\\\\\\t    (or (when-some [o (get (:filed-cursors @link) k)]\\\\\\\\n   530\\\\\\\\t          (swap! link update :filed-cursors dissoc k)\\\\\\\\n   531\\\\\\\\t          (filed-answer-outcome o))\\\\\\\\n   532\\\\\\\\t        (when-some [[id entry]\\\\\\\\n   533\\\\\\\\t                    (filed-for link refl :dao.stream/next c)]\\\\\\\\n   534\\\\\\\\t          (swap! link update :filed dissoc id)\\\\\\\\n   535\\\\\\\\t          (filed-answer-outcome (:ans entry))))))\\\\\\\\n   536\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   537\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   538\\\\\\\\t(defn- retry-read\\\\\\\\n   539\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The answer a just-sent cursor request gives: the source's map,\\\\\\\\n   540\\\\\\\\t   invalid-anchor included, arrives later as an answer.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   541\\\\\\\\t  []\\\\\\\\n   542\\\\\\\\t  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\\\\\\\\n   543\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream/retry? true})\\\\\\\\n   544\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   545\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   546\\\\\\\\t(defn- send-or-count!\\\\\\\\n   547\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Send the request for `op` with `arg` when none of this reflection's\\\\\\\\n   548\\\\\\\\t   own is outstanding; otherwise count one further ask of the\\\\\\\\n   549\\\\\\\\t   outstanding request, which re-sends it at the link's resend-after\\\\\\\\n   550\\\\\\\\t   k.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   551\\\\\\\\t  [link refl op arg]\\\\\\\\n   552\\\\\\\\t  (if-some [id (outstanding-for link refl op arg)]\\\\\\\\n   553\\\\\\\\t    (count-ask! link id)\\\\\\\\n   554\\\\\\\\t    (send-request! link refl (wire-request link refl op [arg]))))\\\\\\\\n   555\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   556\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   557\\\\\\\\t(defn- refl-cursor\\\\\\\\n   558\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The cursor operation: a filed answer for the anchor is returned,\\\\\\\\n   559\\\\\\\\t   and a filed protocol error translated; otherwise a cursor request\\\\\\\\n   560\\\\\\\\t   is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is transport-error\\\\\\\\n   561\\\\\\\\t   with :dao.stream/retry? true. Once closed, cursor answers closed;\\\\\\\\n   562\\\\\\\\t   once gone, transport-error naming not-found.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   563\\\\\\\\t  [refl a]\\\\\\\\n   564\\\\\\\\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   565\\\\\\\\t    (drain! link)\\\\\\\\n   566\\\\\\\\t    (cond\\\\\\\\n   567\\\\\\\\t      (:closed? @refl) (result :dao.stream/closed)\\\\\\\\n   568\\\\\\\\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\\\\\\\\n   569\\\\\\\\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/cursor)\\\\\\\\n   570\\\\\\\\t      (translated :dao.stream.remote/no-surface)\\\\\\\\n   571\\\\\\\\t      :else\\\\\\\\n   572\\\\\\\\t      (or (filed-cursor link refl a)\\\\\\\\n   573\\\\\\\\t          (when-not (:channel-gone? @link)\\\\\\\\n   574\\\\\\\\t            (send-or-count! link refl :dao.stream/cursor a)\\\\\\\\n   575\\\\\\\\t            (retry-read))\\\\\\\\n   576\\\\\\\\t          (translated :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone)))))\\\\\\\\n   577\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   578\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   579\\\\\\\\t(defn- refl-next\\\\\\\\n   580\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The next operation: a filed answer for the cursor is returned and\\\\\\\\n   581\\\\\\\\t   forgotten; otherwise a next request is sent if none is outstanding,\\\\\\\\n   582\\\\\\\\t   and the answer is blocked -- nothing is observable at this position\\\\\\\\n   583\\\\\\\\t   through this handle yet. After close, filed outcomes then end. The\\\\\\\\n   584\\\\\\\\t   reflection never judges a cursor: cursor-mismatch and\\\\\\\\n   585\\\\\\\\t   invalid-cursor are only ever relayed from the source.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   586\\\\\\\\t  [refl c]\\\\\\\\n   587\\\\\\\\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   588\\\\\\\\t    (drain! link)\\\\\\\\n   589\\\\\\\\t    (cond\\\\\\\\n   590\\\\\\\\t      (:closed? @refl)\\\\\\\\n   591\\\\\\\\t      (or (filed-next! link refl c) (result :dao.stream/end))\\\\\\\\n   592\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   593\\\\\\\\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\\\\\\\\n   594\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   595\\\\\\\\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/next)\\\\\\\\n   596\\\\\\\\t      (translated :dao.stream.remote/no-surface)\\\\\\\\n   597\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   598\\\\\\\\t      :else\\\\\\\\n   599\\\\\\\\t      (or (filed-next! link refl c)\\\\\\\\n   600\\\\\\\\t          (when-not (:channel-gone? @link)\\\\\\\\n   601\\\\\\\\t            (send-or-count! link refl :dao.stream/next c)\\\\\\\\n   602\\\\\\\\t            (result :dao.stream/blocked))\\\\\\\\n   603\\\\\\\\t          (translated :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone)))))\\\\\\\\n   604\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   605\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   606\\\\\\\\t(defn- refl-append\\\\\\\\n   607\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The append! operation: send the request and return the channel\\\\\\\\n   608\\\\\\\\t   writer's outcome -- ok is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly\\\\\\\\n   609\\\\\\\\t   the contract's Writing rule for a transport that carries values\\\\\\\\n   610\\\\\\\\t   toward a stream elsewhere. The source's outcome is emitted on the\\\\\\\\n   611\\\\\\\\t   event writer when it is filed, correlated by id; append! never\\\\\\\\n   612\\\\\\\\t   answers the source's full. Only an accepted send is in flight: a\\\\\\\\n   613\\\\\\\\t   refused append! never crossed, so its effect is not unknown.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   614\\\\\\\\t  [refl v]\\\\\\\\n   615\\\\\\\\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   616\\\\\\\\t    (drain! link)\\\\\\\\n   617\\\\\\\\t    (cond\\\\\\\\n   618\\\\\\\\t      (:closed? @refl) (result :dao.stream/closed)\\\\\\\\n   619\\\\\\\\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\\\\\\\\n   620\\\\\\\\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/append!)\\\\\\\\n   621\\\\\\\\t      (translated :dao.stream.remote/no-surface)\\\\\\\\n   622\\\\\\\\t      :else\\\\\\\\n   623\\\\\\\\t      (let [req (wire-request link refl :dao.stream/append! [v])\\\\\\\\n   624\\\\\\\\t            r (stream/append! (:writer @link) req)]\\\\\\\\n   625\\\\\\\\t        (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\\\\\\\\n   626\\\\\\\\t          (register! link refl req))\\\\\\\\n   627\\\\\\\\t        r))))\\\\\\\\n   628\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   629\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   630\\\\\\\\t(defn- refl-close\\\\\\\\n   631\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The close! operation, local: the reflection forgets its\\\\\\\\n   632\\\\\\\\t   outstanding ids, reporting each outstanding append! on the event\\\\\\\\n   633\\\\\\\\t   writer as append-unknown -- an append whose answer never arrives\\\\\\\\n   634\\\\\\\\t   has unknown effect; afterwards append! answers closed, cursor\\\\\\\\n   635\\\\\\\\t   answers closed, next answers filed outcomes then end, and nothing\\\\\\\\n   636\\\\\\\\t   crosses the wire. Idempotent, per the contract's Close.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   637\\\\\\\\t  [refl]\\\\\\\\n   638\\\\\\\\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   639\\\\\\\\t    (drain! link)\\\\\\\\n   640\\\\\\\\t    (when-not (:closed? @refl)\\\\\\\\n   641\\\\\\\\t      (doseq [id (:ids @refl)]\\\\\\\\n   642\\\\\\\\t        (when-some [entry (get (:outstanding @link) id)]\\\\\\\\n   643\\\\\\\\t          (when (= :dao.stream/append! (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req entry)))\\\\\\\\n   644\\\\\\\\t            (emit! link {:dao.stream.remote/event\\\\\\\\n   645\\\\\\\\t                         :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\\\\\\\\n   646\\\\\\\\t                         :dao.stream.remote/id id}))\\\\\\\\n   647\\\\\\\\t          (swap! link update :outstanding dissoc id)))\\\\\\\\n   648\\\\\\\\t      (swap! refl assoc :closed? true :ids #{}))\\\\\\\\n   649\\\\\\\\t    (result :dao.stream/ok)))\\\\\\\\n   650\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   651\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   652\\\\\\\\t(deftype ReflectionHandle\\\\\\\\n   653\\\\\\\\t  [state]\\\\\\\\n   654\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   655\\\\\\\\t  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\\\\\\\\n   656\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   657\\\\\\\\t  (descriptor\\\\\\\\n   658\\\\\\\\t    [_]\\\\\\\\n   659\\\\\\\\t    (refl-descriptor state))\\\\\\\\n   660\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   661\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   662\\\\\\\\t  stream/IDaoStreamReader\\\\\\\\n   663\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   664\\\\\\\\t  (cursor\\\\\\\\n   665\\\\\\\\t    [_ anchor]\\\\\\\\n   666\\\\\\\\t    (refl-cursor state anchor))\\\\\\\\n   667\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   668\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   669\\\\\\\\t  (next\\\\\\\\n   670\\\\\\\\t    [_ c]\\\\\\\\n   671\\\\\\\\t    (refl-next state c))\\\\\\\\n   672\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   673\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   674\\\\\\\\t  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\\\\\\\\n   675\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   676\\\\\\\\t  (append!\\\\\\\\n   677\\\\\\\\t    [_ v]\\\\\\\\n   678\\\\\\\\t    (refl-append state v))\\\\\\\\n   679\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   680\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   681\\\\\\\\t  stream/IDaoStreamClosable\\\\\\\\n   682\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   683\\\\\\\\t  (close!\\\\\\\\n   684\\\\\\\\t    [_]\\\\\\\\n   685\\\\\\\\t    (refl-close state)))\\\\\\\\n   686\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   687\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   688\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n   689\\\\\\\\t;; The remote descriptor dispatch (2.2)\\\\\\\\n   690\\\\\\\\t;; =============================================================================\\\\\\\\n   691\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   692\\\\\\\\t(defn- valid-remote-descriptor?\\\\\\\\n   693\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"A remote descriptor names a stream and a channel: the generic\\\\\\\\n   694\\\\\\\\t   envelope rules, the :dao.stream/remote type, and a channel\\\\\\\\n   695\\\\\\\\t   descriptor. It carries no authorization, no surface and no\\\\\\\\n   696\\\\\\\\t   anchors; the mirror answers those.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   697\\\\\\\\t  [d]\\\\\\\\n   698\\\\\\\\t  (and (stream/valid-descriptor? d)\\\\\\\\n   699\\\\\\\\t       (= :dao.stream/remote (:dao.stream/type d))\\\\\\\\n   700\\\\\\\\t       (contains? d :dao.stream/channel)))\\\\\\\\n   701\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   702\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n   703\\\\\\\\t(defn attacher\\\\\\\\n   704\\\\\\\\t  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The composition's :dao.stream/attach entry for :dao.stream/remote\\\\\\\\n   705\\\\\\\\t   descriptors; a dynamic dispatch table has this entry and no\\\\\\\\n   706\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream/create one, because attaching through this transport\\\\\\\\n   707\\\\\\\\t   never creates. `opts` carries the composition data:\\\\\\\\n   708\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream.remote/channels maps each channel descriptor this peer\\\\\\\\n   709\\\\\\\\t   reaches to its own end {:reader r :writer w} -- two ring buffers in\\\\\\\\n   710\\\\\\\\t   one process today, the ws, udp and pair channels of section 3\\\\\\\\n   711\\\\\\\\t   later; :dao.stream.remote/events is the optional event writer the\\\\\\\\n   712\\\\\\\\t   links emit on; :dao.stream.remote/resend-after is the links'\\\\\\\\n   713\\\\\\\\t   resend-after k, unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small\\\\\\\\n   714\\\\\\\\t   on udp; :dao.stream.remote/budget is the budget stamped on every\\\\\\\\n   715\\\\\\\\t   next request. One link is kept per channel descriptor, shared by\\\\\\\\n   716\\\\\\\\t   every reflection through that channel. attach! answers ok at once\\\\\\\\n   717\\\\\\\\t   with a reflection handle and a local, opaque\\\\\\\\n   718\\\\\\\\t   :dao.stream/attachment -- the contract's deferred remote\\\\\\\\n   719\\\\\\\\t   confirmation -- and the link sends one descriptor probe for the\\\\\\\\n   720\\\\\\\\t   identity: its answer is the reflection's confirmation, not-found\\\\\\\\n   721\\\\\\\\t   marking the reflection gone, ok recording the source's descriptor\\\\\\\\n   722\\\\\\\\t   and declared surface.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n   723\\\\\\\\t  [opts]\\\\\\\\n   724\\\\\\\\t  (let [channels (:dao.stream.remote/channels opts)\\\\\\\\n   725\\\\\\\\t        policy (select-keys opts [:dao.stream.remote/events\\\\\\\\n   726\\\\\\\\t                                  :dao.stream.remote/resend-after\\\\\\\\n   727\\\\\\\\t                                  :dao.stream.remote/budget])\\\\\\\\n   728\\\\\\\\t        links (atom {})]\\\\\\\\n   729\\\\\\\\t    (fn [descriptor]\\\\\\\\n   730\\\\\\\\t      (if-not (valid-remote-descriptor? descriptor)\\\\\\\\n   731\\\\\\\\t        (result :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor)\\\\\\\\n   732\\\\\\\\t        (let [cd (:dao.stream/channel descriptor)]\\\\\\\\n   733\\\\\\\\t          (if-not (contains? channels cd)\\\\\\\\n   734\\\\\\\\t            (result :dao.stream/not-found)\\\\\\\\n   735\\\\\\\\t            (do (swap! links\\\\\\\\n   736\\\\\\\\t                       (fn [m]\\\\\\\\n   737\\\\\\\\t                         (if (contains? m cd)\\\\\\\\n   738\\\\\\\\t                           m\\\\\\\\n   739\\\\\\\\t                           (assoc m cd\\\\\\\\n   740\\\\\\\\t                                  (new-link (get channels cd) policy)))))\\\\\\\\n   741\\\\\\\\t                (let [link (get @links cd)\\\\\\\\n   742\\\\\\\\t                      refl (atom {:link link\\\\\\\\n   743\\\\\\\\t                                  :descriptor descriptor\\\\\\\\n   744\\\\\\\\t                                  :identity (:dao.stream/identity\\\\\\\\n   745\\\\\\\\t                                              descriptor)\\\\\\\\n   746\\\\\\\\t                                  :surface nil\\\\\\\\n   747\\\\\\\\t                                  :source-descriptor nil\\\\\\\\n   748\\\\\\\\t                                  :gone? false\\\\\\\\n   749\\\\\\\\t                                  :closed? false\\\\\\\\n   750\\\\\\\\t                                  :ids #{}})]\\\\\\\\n   751\\\\\\\\t                  (send-request!\\\\\\\\n   752\\\\\\\\t                    link refl\\\\\\\\n   753\\\\\\\\t                    (wire-request link refl :dao.stream/descriptor []))\\\\\\\\n   754\\\\\\\\t                  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\\\\\\\\n   755\\\\\\\\t                   :dao.stream/handle (ReflectionHandle. refl)\\\\\\\\n   756\\\\\\\\t                   :dao.stream/attachment (str (random-uuid))}))))))))\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n./collab/1790479176072-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-confirm.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:34:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_17\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-27 03:20:25 GMT\\\\\\\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-27 10:20:25 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nP1 | [remote.cljc:751](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:751) | A `full` refusal leaves the attach descriptor probe unregistered, as intended by [send-request!](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:279). But [drain!](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:446) retries only *outstanding* probes. Once the writer recovers, no later operation sends that probe: remote confirmation, the learned surface, and its confirmation event can remain absent indefinitely. The new test at [remote_test.cljc:583](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc:583) checks cursor recovery but not probe recovery. | Retain a pending unsent probe and retry it on a later drain; add a test that refuses the probe, then confirms its answer after writer recovery.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe cursor and next refusal fix is sound on inspection. Installed outcomes use the answer’s served identity and cursor, matching the consuming reflection’s identity and cursor for normal mirror answers. I accepted the supplied JVM, Node, and Dart results without rerunning suites.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\\\\\\\\nSign-off: DENIED\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n./collab/1790476870000-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2-fixes.glm-flash.report.md:4:`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:278-289`: `send-request!` now calls `register!` only when the writer answered `:dao.stream/ok`. A send refused with `full` leaves the request unsent and nothing outstanding, so `outstanding-for` (remote.cljc:549) does not strand the next ask — the retry cases re-send on the next ask, exactly per 2.4's \\\\\\\"leaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered\\\\\\\". This covers the attach probe (remote.cljc:823) and cursor/next sends; `refl-append` (remote.cljc:620-628) already registered only accepted appends and is untouched.\\\\n./collab/1790476861675-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-gate.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:4:P1 | [remote.cljc:278](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:278) | `send-request!` registers a `descriptor`, `cursor`, or `next` request even when the channel writer refuses it with `full`. Later calls find that unsent request outstanding ([line 543](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:543)); with no `resend-after`, they never send it. A transient full channel can therefore strand the attach probe or a read indefinitely. | Register only accepted sends, or retry a refused send on the next ask.\\\\n./collab/1790479176072-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-confirm.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:4:P1 | [remote.cljc:751](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:751) | A `full` refusal leaves the attach descriptor probe unregistered, as intended by [send-request!](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:279). But [drain!](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:446) retries only *outstanding* probes. Once the writer recovers, no later operation sends that probe: remote confirmation, the learned surface, and its confirmation event can remain absent indefinitely. The new test at [remote_test.cljc:583](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc:583) checks cursor recovery but not probe recovery. | Retain a pending unsent probe and retry it on a later drain; add a test that refuses the probe, then confirms its answer after writer recovery.\\\\n./collab/1790479176072-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-confirm.gpt-6-sol.prompt.md:16:1. send-request! (:278-289) registers only when the writer answered\\\\n./test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc:616:(deftest a-refused-probe-is-kept-and-retried-on-a-later-drain\\\\n./collab/1790481481207-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-confirm-r2.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:8:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_3\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && rg -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"retry-pending!|send-request!|defn.*drain!|refl-close|a-refused-probe-is-kept\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" .'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n./collab/1790476861675-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice2-gate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:7:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rg -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"dao.stream.remote|mirror-step|reflection|link|2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.1|2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.2|2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.3|2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.4|2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" docs/design/datom.world.md docs src test collab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"collab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:3:**`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`** (new, 767 lines) — the only source file touched:\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:5:- **Wire shapes (2.1)**: `wire-ops` (exactly the four ops), `protocol-errors` (the three names), `well-formed-request?`/`well-formed-answer?`, `required-surface` (2.1's fixed mapping: cursor/next need `:reader`, `append!` needs `:writer`, descriptor needs none), `error-answer` (remote.cljc:27-140).\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:6:- **Mirror step (2.3)**: `mirror-step` (remote.cljc:234-256) reads the channel reader to blocked/end with the caller's cursor, skips malformed values, and `answer!` (remote.cljc:183-232) runs the four-step order: table lookup -> `not-found`; declared-surface check -> `no-surface`; descriptor answered from the entry's handle *directly* (never apply-request) with `:dao.stream.remote/surface`; cursor/next/append! through `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request` with context `{:dao.stream.remote/channel ...}`; step 4 appends the answer with id + identity. The budget chase (`chase`, remote.cljc:160-180) places up to k-1 further outcome maps in order under `:dao.stream.remote/more`, ending at and including the first non-ok; `write-answer!` (remote.cljc:166-178) answers `oversize` when the channel writer refuses the answer with `invalid-value`. The mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, anchor or outcome.\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:7:- **Link (2.4, 2.5)**: `new-link` (remote.cljc:266-282) holds writer, reader + own cursor, outstanding ids with the request each was sent for, filed answers keyed by id, more-outcomes installed at the cursor that precedes each (`install-more!`, remote.cljc:364-374), optional event writer, `resend-after`. `drain!` (remote.cljc:441-461) reads to blocked; `end` runs `channel-loss!` (remote.cljc:418-433): outstanding ids abandoned, each abandoned append! reported `append-unknown`, later cursor/next answered `transport-error` reason `channel-gone`, append! keeps the writer's own outcome, filed answers still returned. `count-ask!` (remote.cljc:293-302) re-sends an outstanding idempotent request at the k-th further ask; append! is never re-sent and only an accepted send counts as in flight.\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:8:- **Reflection (2.4)**: `ReflectionHandle` (remote.cljc:660-690) — descriptor local (always `ok`, even gone/closed); cursor (retry-read with `:dao.stream/retry? true` on send); next (filed returned and forgotten, else send and `blocked`); append! (returns the channel writer's outcome verbatim); close! (local, forgets outstanding ids with append-unknown events, then append!/cursor `closed`, next filed-then-end, idempotent). Protocol errors translate to `transport-error` with the error's name (`translated`); `not-found` marks gone at filing (descriptor still answers ok); refused returned verbatim, marks nothing; the learned surface (probe's `surface` + `:closable`, pre-probe `#{:reader :writer :closable}`) answers no-surface locally; next only ever relays cursor-mismatch/invalid-cursor; append! never answers the source's `full` (event writer only).\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:9:- **Dispatch (2.2)**: `attacher` (remote.cljc:712-767) is the composition's `:dao.stream/attach` entry with no `:dao.stream/create`; validates the remote descriptor (`invalid-descriptor`), answers `not-found` for an unreachable channel descriptor, keeps one link per channel descriptor shared by every reflection, answers `ok` at once with handle + opaque attachment (deferred confirmation), and sends the descriptor probe.\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:11:**`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc`** (new, 946 lines) — 18 deftests covering the whole proof row plus the brief's items: `the-toy-over-two-ring-buffers` (probe shape, local descriptor, retry-then-ok `:oldest`, blocked-then-ok, end, kept cursor accepted by the source handle), `gap-from-an-evicting-source-crosses-verbatim`, `not-found-marks-gone`, `no-surface-marks-nothing`, `the-descriptor-answer-carries-the-surface`, `the-budget-chase` + `...-ends-at-the-first-non-ok-outcome` + `without-a-budget-no-chase-runs`, `an-uncarryable-answer-is-oversize`, `resend-after-resends-an-unanswered-request` (dropping toy writer), `channel-loss` (append-unknown, channel-gone, filed still returned, writer's closed), `refused-is-returned-verbatim-and-marks-nothing`, `close-semantics` (3 branches), `next-relays-position-errors-and-never-judges`, `answers-with-unknown-ids-are-dropped`, `the-mirror-step-answers-the-four-ops-in-order` (wire-level four-step order, verbatim relay, `:more` in order, malformed input dropped), `the-mirror-applies-requests-with-the-channel-context` (gate sees the channel; allow-list end-to-end, refusal stays with its own reflection), `the-remote-descriptor-dispatches`.\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:16:2. **Budget minting**: the spec fixes the wire key but not who mints it; the link composes the requests, so `:dao.stream.remote/budget` is attacher composition data stamped on every `next` request. Absence is always correct (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a mirror that ignores the budget is correct\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\").\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:18:4. **Channel-reader gap** (mirror and link): adopt the reader's own recovery cursor and stop; lost wire values are lost, resend recovers requests.\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:19:5. **Filing scope**: a filed answer keeps its request and its reflection — an answer belongs to the operation that caused it (refusal is per operation), so reflections on one link never consume each other's answers; `:more` installations stay link-level shared positional truth. `cursor` filings are kept (the spec says \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forgotten\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" only for `next`); `outstanding-for`/`filed-for` both correlate per (reflection, op, arg).\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:23:9. **Verbatim return**: the envelope's correlation keys (`:dao.stream.remote/id`, `:dao.stream/identity`) are stripped so the caller receives the source's outcome map itself.\\\\\\\\ncollab/1790471994616-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice2.glm-flash.report.md:24:10. The reflection implements all four protocols (the surface is learned only from the probe); the learned surface reproduces the translated no-surface answer exactly. No `dao.stream.md` change was required — the deferred-confirmation clause and `:dao.stream/refused` were already in the contract.\\\\\\\\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:18:3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\\\\\\\ntest/datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff_test.cljc:21:  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The composition's capability secret (yin.vm.linker.md 7.3, r10).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ntest/datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff_test.cljc:266:              :tail? false, ; §2.4: the datom loader saturates the default\\\\\\\\ndocs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:246:   `dao.stream.remote.md` (see its implementation plan). Both handles\\\\\\\\ntest/yang/clojure/stream_eval_test.cljc:421:        ;; a reference's seal is its task's (yin.vm.linker.md 7.3, r10):\\\\\\\\ntest/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn:11:;;   :cljs  @noble/hashes 2.4.0 (Node and browser Shadow-CLJS builds)\\\\\\\\ntest/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn:36:             :cljs \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"@noble/hashes 2.4.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2124:method and compiled call sites link straight to the interface, bypassing the\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2296:  public-facing product regression); a public link\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2310:  routes as aliases into the `-v2` picker entries so the public link\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2641:    3. Hot-loop twins: Named the 3 inlined sites in `ast_walker.cljc` (lines 540-543, 580-585, 639-646) (§2.3).\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2642:    4. Literal validation: Closed per-node vocabulary, recursive plain-data check on values, no `:vm/*` glob, `:vm/store-update` excluded (§2.4).\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2648:  - Additional review points adopted: transaction-local vs durable identity (§2.7), walker loader `:keep-eids?` predicate (§3.6), fuel/cycles/parked-body checks (§3.2, §3.3), audit of denied expansion attempts (§3.3, §4.1), portable snapshot (§6.4), shadow hints as facts (§2.1, §2.5).\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2693:  - Ran `--version` across both wrappers; verified clean exit 0 (`2.1.270`).\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2826:One refactor landed (4efdf9a): `yin.vm.stream-observer` → `dao.stream.observer`, API made consumer-neutral (`run-on-stream` over `{:observer :consumer}` with `ready? load run`). Tests: JVM 106/440 green; Node 1223/34285 with 5 pre-existing `dao.stream.slice-test` failures (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"process B never replied :ready\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", spawn/handshake of build/slice-peer under Node; the same exe passes on the JVM); cljd compiles. `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` Open items → \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Incremental indexing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" links to the note.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2839:**Step 3 — Index note Phase 0′** (`dao.space.index`: `fold-batch`, `publish!`, `flush-staged`, `drain`, `db-value`, `coverage`, `checkpoint`, `restore`, the admitting element rule and outer grammar, resolution facts) against a transactor medium in `:resolved` mode. The acceptance list is in the note's Phase 0′ paragraph and is long on purpose; glm verified each mechanism claim against `dao/data/btree.cljc` (`conj`, `store-tree` dirty-subgraph, `restore-tree`, `walk-addresses`, ref types incl. the `:test` seam) so the implementer can trust those citations. Watch: §3.1 mode matrix and §2.2 step 0 grammar (both reviewers flagged them as the sections needing closest line-level attention); `:strong` must be on the *tree's* settings for both fresh and resumed sessions (glm N1); parity with `publish-index!` is logical equality, never manifest equality (astra A9).\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2933:Tree: dao.stream-redesign-v2@1af3b73, uncommitted: docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md (§2.2 sync + §7 pointer), docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md (r2+r3, 1331 lines)\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2945:Done: Interactive design exploration of the owner's ruling that the Universal AST becomes flat tag-first positional tuples (Erlang abstract format shape) for Unison-style content-hashing, walker semantics unchanged. Verified the mechanics locally: dispatch surface is ast_walker.cljc:359/:600 + linearize.cljc:91/:234 (all on (:type node) — mechanical rewrite); codec already saturates defaults (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/1024, v2.cljc:444/453); ast->datoms already throws on :vm/store-update (host :fn in syntax — outside persistent form today) and carries a :stream/close arm the walker lacks; the AST is already a DAG via pre-assigned :eid sharing (v2.cljc:394-411) — Unison sharing-by-content-address slots in exactly there. Grammar drafted: tag-first, fixed arity per tag, operand vectors for application/call, tail? as saturated trailing boolean (mirrors segment [:call argc tail?]), no source positions or :macro? in canonical form (side tables keyed by AST address). Merkle grain ruled: whole-tree first (all named payoffs work at that grain), per-lambda subterm addressing as follow-up. Verified live that dao.space.query/q performs Datalog over the flat per-node tuple projection [id tag & slots] with no datom intermediary: general where-path is exact-arity positional unification (eval-pattern-clause→unify-slots, query.cljc:894/799); the 3-slot EAV fast path (:899) only engages for explicit fact-relation values. Demonstrated: per-tag queries, 3-clause joins across mixed arities, pc-prepended segment rows, DAG-sharing join (#{[1] [4]}). Owner ruling: the semantic VM is designed around this — tuples are the canonical code form at both levels, datoms demoted to storage/index projection, tuple loading primary (both-paths-same-image as the VM's own conformance test), validation = the tuple grammar, UCF §7.6.1 dependency fixed point recast as Datalog over the same tuples, hot loop never queries. Closes the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"code-on-stream tuples vs datoms end-to-end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" open item: tuples end-to-end. Committed docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md per explicit owner instruction (3325815: §2.2 :yin.code/hash typed as segment address of the canonical instruction vector; §7 pointer to the extracted UCF doc).\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2967:Done: Round-3 revision (fable 8677b374, resumed; 1132→1352 lines): all 8 round-2 findings addressed, none overruled — encoder gate WITHDRAWN categorically (every identity/dedup use blocked until dao.jing codec fix; addresses only computed/carried for conformance; list/seq/metadata counterexamples tabled), key widened to the full data domain, store-update result condition excludes effect descriptors, occurrence origins ([:source medium batch] / [:expansion event-id]) added, ordered batch + stray-mark rejection, per-context name obligations (§7.7.2) + footprint table (§7.7.1), dedicated lowering profile (§5.2.1/5.2.2) with structured stamp and separated integrity/derivation verification. Round-3 review (astra 01a09fcc, no usage abort this time): REJECT, 6 P1s — 4/8 resolved (key domain, store-update condition, encoder withdrawal — \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"categorical\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", lowering profile — \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"closes the revision ambiguity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"), mnemonic normalization itself verified correct against execution. Remaining: (1) batch member index missing from source occurrence keys (declarations carry j, provenance doesn't — identical trees at j=0/j=1 still conflated); (2) [:expansion ev-id] embedded in an occurrence value is NOT transactor-resolved (astra executed: nested tempid in a vector v passes through; apply-tempid-map resolves only whole-v tempids on declared ref attrs — orchestrator verified at transact.cljc:168-185) and a bare log-local event number doesn't identify its log when the record travels — needs a separate declared reference attribute + qualified log/event identity; (3) preorder ≠ arbitrary admitted batch order — the legacy contract admits batches whose entity order differs from structural order, changing the winning duplicate-name definition; needs an explicit ordered admission catalogue or an explicit narrowing amendment; (4) static parameter discharge assumes exact arity — zipmap under-arity leaves params unbound (astra executed: closure [x] called with 0 args → env {} → x resolves to primitive; orchestrator verified apply-call at semantic.cljc:199-205); (5) the fixed point converges on ADDRESSES but obligations are per-CONTEXT — two closures sharing one address with different captured envs; convergence must be over dependency facts incl. code/context pairs, else :incomplete; (6) REGRESSED: the new FFI footprint adds call-in/call-out keys to the store-slice requirement, contradicting UCF §7.6.2's explicit exclusion (pair is receiver-local capability, not transported state). §10 inventory judged substantially improved but presents occurrence identity/batch preservation/conservative completion as ready-to-implement when design work remains.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:2995:- 2026-09-15 02:26 +07: §1 Content cell 'Code as tuples' → 'The code itself, in its canonical tuple form'; §2.1 opens by fixing the ontology — the tuple tree IS the AST in canonical form, not a projection (the flat rows and datom batch are the projections). §1 table rebuilt merge-aware at width 170.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:3005:(the r7 text — STALE in §2.1, see below) and docs/agents/file-format.md with the\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:3017:linked lists\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", then, stepwise: the walker's map AST is the semantic layer (the\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:3056:3. **The §2.3 grammar table becomes the projection/reconstruction dictionary.**\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:3068:6. **Frontends keep emitting map ASTs**; the strip(§2.5)/saturate(§2.4)/\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:3077:ontology: its §2.1 says the NESTED tuple tree IS the AST and the walker is\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:3079:quotes, seven constraints, a section-by-section delta map (§2.1 invert, §2.3\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:3284:`syms` kind in §2.2's dictionary, misleading migration-surface citations, and\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:3396:yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md, all 18 §2.3 grammar tags, round-trip law over a\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:4316:themselves (`dao.stream.{apply,file,http,link,ringbuffer,udp,ws}`,\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:4893:§2.4's explicit mapping list, and the `:call` row cited §4.3 (commentary)\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:4895:execution contract's revision history as a new note in §2.4, satisfying\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5460:- U3: `ast-walker/vm-load-rows` implemented. Fixed `datoms->ast` to saturate `:tail?` for `:application` per UCF §2.4, ensuring exact image equivalence for the walker between row and datom lanes.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5462:- U7: `occurrences` emitted structurally, and root-scoped Datalog rules established for `dao.space.query` integration. Path prefixes conform to §2.5 exactly.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5768:Done: Four interactive units at the user's direction, all committed and (except the last two blog commits) pushed. (1) dao.lease contract: the user's grid-table reflow committed verbatim as 775937e0; the two-architect contract mob (gpt-5.6-sol × fable-5-1, three rounds) ruled the open §6 questions and the rulings landed as 8d14ffda — Q1: an unknown lease is due for :silence only when BOTH the known bound AND a full duration since the resumed reading hold (a gap may delay a reclaim, never speed one up); Q2: the holder's cap basis is its observed grant and fencing, not tolerance, covers the judge/holder flight-time gap; Q3: the waitset plan's tentative latency note replaced with the accepted composition-policy wording. The four Q1-adjacent plan-only rules (truncated-drain silence suppression, the 2^52 exactness bound, fail-closed unusable readings, sizing strict-half) were promoted from the transient plan into dao.lease.md as 7f5c21ef so the plan can be retired without loss. (2) Site characters/icons (a5843ee0, 627b154a, 660e9a9a, fbba6785, c8baa576, 4e648d63, df0586fb, f0efd65c, 04f6dae9): each dao-* page gained an h1 Chinese character — 井 confirmed for DaoJing by dao-jing.chp's own text, 租 for DaoLease, 道空 for DaoSpace, 道 leading every dao-* h1; the DaoLease page itself (借道 h1, four-cause table, anti-spoofing section, JIT+GC essay link) landed in this batch on master after the user's branch-history rework folded the earlier ada8ea20 lineage in. (3) Blog rewrites for the tuple era (613dd7d6, 75cae8f9, ca04467f): universal-ast-vs-assembly rewritten around semantic tuples rather than datoms, the projection-as-tuples post, and the content-addressed index-segments post — aligning the public prose with the de Bruijn/segment design thread.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5798:Done: Phase D2 (node hashes, hash-consing, :yin.debruijn/* records, d5 storage adapter) landed after build → review → fix → confirmation → sign-off. Build (glm-5.3 resumed, brief 1789932323761): descriptor-order tag-specific hashing with :hash/:root excluded, fingerprint = hash(root), records keyed by hash, pure in-namespace adapter projected->datoms / datoms->projected (one entity per hash, deterministic root-down handles, :operands one ordered vector datom, root marker on the fingerprint entity, reader diagnostics for malformed/missing/duplicate hashes, dangling links, non-vector operands). Review (claude-fable-5-1, 1789933291780): NOT READY — P1: the consing memo keyed by Clojure = merged list literals with vectors ((= '(1 2) [1 2]) is true) and 0.0 with -0.0 (reliably on CLJS), so identity depended on traversal order and non-equivalent programs could share a fingerprint; 2 P2 (ident parts bypassed the NFC seam; the reader never verified records hash to their address). Fix round (glm-5.3 resumed, 1789933440000-era brief 1789933291780-adjacent — actual prompt 1789932917… see collab/ *d2-fixr2*): ORCHESTRATOR RULING option (b) — memo re-keyed on the node's full preimage, which separates exactly what the hash separates; ident parts routed through normalize-nfc; reader recomputes each record hash (:hash-mismatch) with a string? guard (:malformed-hash); provisional int64 content renders via (str (long v)) so 1 ≡ 1.0 on every host; new tests: list-beside-vector and ±0.0 in one program, decomposed≡composed free names, tampered-scalar mismatch, 1≡1.0 fingerprint. Confirmation (claude-fable-5-1, *d2-confirm*): READY, all resolved; two new P3s dispositioned by phase — D3 must canonicalize scalars inside records (long, NFC) so one address holds one content; D4 must order reader diagnostics so wrong-typed slots diagnose rather than raise (hash check currently precedes type checks). Sign-off (gpt-5.6-sol resumed 01a0bacb…, 1789934100841): GRANTED — preimage-keyed consing proven sound, adapter shape blessed for D5, carried P3s are required D3/D4 completion work.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5959:Done: (1) DaoJing CBOR epic opened at the owner's word on its own worktree, scoped to steps 1-2 only (frozen fixtures + Boring JVM/JS codec + Dart codec + three-host conformance, purely additive). Fresh gpt-5.6-sol architect thread 01a0c501-5311-71f0-94e5-033950e0473d (artifacts 1790011562103-*, 1790011825648-*, 1790012238375-* addendum). Findings: dao.stream.cbor already landed (commit 4cdd924e, Boring 0.1.30 pinned in deps.edn, pub.dev cbor 6.5.1 in pubspec.yaml) so dependency work is stale; dao.jing.cbor is a SIBLING namespace reusing pins and patterns, NOT the stream profile (dao.stream/list vs dao.jing/list, tag 39 vs component frames, metadata policy, numerics differ); phases J0 fixtures, J1 JVM/Node, J2 Dart, J3 conformance; Eve flat vs CBOR ruled CBOR (Eve lists no Dart, no canonical profile, unstable, no independent readers); dao.data.btree.md:700 still says \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Eve flat, per dao.jing.dht.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (stale; replacement wording ready, NOT applied); dao.jing.cbor.md needs a status sentence separating the landed stream codec from unbuilt Jing storage encoding and a supersedes-Eve note (wording ready, NOT applied). Gates untouched: dao.space comparator sign-off (incl. min/max tie rule) and clean-break rebuild readiness (UNVERIFIED) both still block step 3. No implementer dispatched. (2) The owner said the de Bruijn projection is not finished: they want a VM that runs a linearized de Bruijn encoding alongside the named one. Architect (gpt-5.6-sol thread 01a0bacb-3b91-7190-8412-3f1e85bb552a, artifacts 1790013357432-* design, 1790013563434-* reference-by-hash, 1790014050855-* linker-as-stream, 1790015066685-* revision, 1790015369264-* Unison prior art, 1790015650437-* revision-r2) authored docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md. Independent review by claude-opus-5 (session bbeae799-313d-4447-8264-714c09a5f1ca; artifacts 1790014395419-* r1 NOT READY 5 P1 10 P2 7 P3; 1790015469938-* r2 NOT READY 3 new P1) drove two revisions. Structural finding: no VM running the PROJECTED RECORDS can be equivalent, because the projection is lossy by design (1.0->1, NFC, no tail flags, no param names); the owner's belief that the projection is a bijection was corrected (only the named form is bijective). Ruling: architecture B, a lossless invertible de Bruijn encoding derived from the NAMED datoms with a name table, projection kept as identity layer; P2-F ruling: image derived by adapting linearize/lower output (rewrite :var via public resolve-name, closures carry arity), not a second traversal. r3 confirmation by opus was launched (artifact 1790015887769-*) and is pending. (3) Unison claims fact-checked against primary sources for the blog and the design: hash of syntax tree, De Bruijn in hashing, dependencies as hashes, names as metadata, SQLite schema (object blobs + type/dependents/name indexes), runtime doc pipeline (let-rec minimization, lambda lifting, ANF, IR with De Bruijn indices as stack positions); UNVERIFIED: MCode/Machine, definition resolution at run time, whether runtime consumes the exact stored term.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5960:Decisions: Owner rulings 2026-09-22 (also saved to memory as project-linker-is-stream-boundary): exact Unison runtime interoperability is NOT a goal; the linker's boundary is dao.stream, so local versus remote linking is just a stream and the linker is one transport-agnostic hash-identity linker (not \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Unison-like versus local-only\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"). Still open because streams do not decide them: authoritative name ledger and trust, SCC identity for mutual recursion, retry/timeout/permanent-absence policy. Owner point on lambda lifting: because the compilation pipeline is composed as dao.stream, lambda lifting (and ANF) can be a separate downstream/upstream AST-to-AST stage; deferred, not part of B0-B6; wording for a non-goals note NOT yet sent to the architect. Rejected running the Jing CBOR steps on cmd/qwen (unauthorized destination). Process lessons: my monitors false-alarmed twice (a substring match against a shell command inside the codex JSONL; and jq aborting on one malformed log line reported turn.completed=0 for a finished turn): use `jq -R 'fromjson? ...'` and the bracket trick in pgrep patterns so a check cannot match its own command line; headless claude cannot read outside its launch directory.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5970:Done: docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md (592 lines, status \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"design; not implemented\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") committed as b5f5e78a (docs(design):, no trailers, hook changed nothing). The owner stated the governing invariant (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"I want de Bruijn projection so that a yin.vm can easily share code over dao.stream linker\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"), said they do not care about the individual open questions, and delegated them to the orchestrator and the team (docs/agents/team.md) under datom.world.md's invariants plus that invariant (memory: project-invariant-share-code-over-stream-linker). Process: architect gpt-5.6-sol (thread 01a0bacb-3b91-7190-8412-3f1e85bb552a; artifacts 1790017487131-* decisions, 1790018181171-* r6, earlier 1790015066685/1790015650437/1790016056331/1790016536012/1790017000570-*) authored and revised the design six times; claude-opus-5 (bbeae799-313d-4447-8264-714c09a5f1ca) reviewed five passes (1790014395419, 1790015469938, 1790015887769, 1790016262426, 1790016866497-*); claude-fable-5-1 (50b423bc-219f-40c0-8e56-2b6d9c3d9658) one architectural review (1790016340343-*); deepseek-v4-pro (3c3398fe-74c3-4bfb-bb9b-0a0091d7d777) and qwen/qwen3.8-max via cmd (session 10e55420-8cfe-45ac-b2b3-866bd4507648) independent decision reviews (1790017709976-*), both SOUND WITH CHANGES. Decided (design section 8): D1 normalizer default; D2 drop the lossless source DAG (derive, don't persist; nothing consumed it); D3 descriptor with raw-Bytes exact-spelling slots, common scalar domain, receiver refuses unsupported classes before execution; D4 named-VM environment leak fixed separately outside the B phases (fixture restriction until then); D5/D9 H is one image-hash function defined in B1, Jing segment-key is only the storage address; D6 linker principles (name environment a value or stream, verify by hash, cycles hashed as a unit, retry/absence as stream events); D7 both VMs coexist, benchmark informational, retiring the semantic VM needs its own design; D8 (new, from both independent reviewers) the stream linker is a COMMITTED phase: B6 closed-image fetch-by-hash over dao.stream, B7 dependency closure; D10 scalar classes derived from hashed const tags; D11 pre-B7 an image is safely shareable only if closed (no :load-free operands) or free names bound identically on the receiver.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5971:Decisions: Architecture B (lower from the NAMED datoms, adapt linearize/lower output; the lossy projection stays the alpha-equivalence identity layer) was chosen because reviewers proved no VM running projected records can be equivalent (1.0->1, NFC, no tail flags, no param names); the owner's belief that the projection is a bijection was corrected (only the named form is). Orchestrator made one formatting-only edit to the architect's file: realigned the section 1 compliance table with a script that proved cell text identical (all rows 77 chars). fable was NOT used for the final check: it had ~10% left and resets at 04:00 +07, so its architectural check of the committed design waits until after 04:00 (any change goes in a follow-up commit). GPT weekly limit ~20% left, resets 2026-09-23 20:44 +07 (memory: project-gpt-credits-status); owner authorized GLM, deepseek, cmd qwen, claude, gpt as destinations (memory: project-delegate-routing-authorization); owner briefly considered poolside/laguna-s-2.1 then chose qwen3.8-max. Monitor lessons: never match error TEXT inside delegate logs (a 429 line number in a file read false-alarmed; deepseek/qwen logs contain the design's text); kill a monitor shell by a bracketed pgrep pattern; qwen via cmd emits a 15MB JSON event log whose final report is the type \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" event's finalText.\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5981:Done: The owner argued \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"if we have H, we don't need the projection\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and, after verification that the projection has no consumer in the running system (persist-compiled! has no caller; nothing indexes projected records; the design's linker already used H), ruled: LEAVE THE PROJECTION CODE DORMANT (merged, untouched, no new dependents) and update the design. Architect gpt-5.6-sol (thread 01a0bacb-3b91-7190-8412-3f1e85bb552a; artifact 1790020037057-*) edited docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md: H is the sole image, linker, request, response, verification and cache identity; projection fingerprint removed as lookup index and image metadata; three-artifact model = named datoms, executable image, projection dormant; B5 acceptance no longer references projection identity; decision D12 recorded; B2's reuse of the public resolve-name documented with its retirement condition (move it into this design's namespace before ever retiring the projection); non-goal note on a possible coarse dedupe hash derived from a normalized image. Committed a853dafa (docs(design):). At the owner's approval the one-sentence dormancy note was added to the status paragraph of docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md, committed ca34bfc9. Both commits: no trailers, hook changed nothing (post-commit shasums equal).\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5985:Next: (a) BLOG public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog is now inaccurate after the redesign and after a section \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The Future: A De Bruijn VM Compiler\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" was added (file mtime 2026-09-21 23:30, not by this seat): it says the compiler would lower :yin.debruijn/* tuples directly, that only the fingerprint or nameless tuples need to be transmitted, that Unison's runtime \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"operates exactly like this today\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and delivers \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Unison's execution speed and footprint\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (all contradicted or unverified), calls the work speculative although it is now a committed design (B0-B7), and its status block and section 4 still plan Datalog over projected records and grouping by fingerprint; the yin.vm side needs rewriting around the executable image and H, the projection dormant, the B6 closed-image linker and B7; awaiting the owner's word to rewrite (the Unison-side facts are sourced and fine). (b) fable-5-1 architectural check after 04:00 +07; (c) B0 (test-only normalizer and frozen parity corpus) awaiting the owner's word to dispatch on Claude; (d) parked: DaoJing CBOR epic, push (8 commits ahead), commit this log, archive collab/ (about 85 artifacts).\\\\\\\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:5992:Done: At the owner's word rewrote the yin.vm side of public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog, which was inaccurate after the redesign and after a section \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The Future: A De Bruijn VM Compiler\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" had been added outside this seat (file mtime 2026-09-21 23:30). Replaced: the status block (dated 2026-09-22: Implemented = named AST datoms, lowering to :yin.code/* run by the semantic VM, name-based content-addressed code via yin.vm.content, and the merged but DORMANT projection tested on JVM/JS/Dart; Designed, not yet implemented = de Bruijn VM over an executable image derived from the NAMED datoms, image hash H ignoring binder names, sharing over dao.stream, fetch-by-hash linker, phases B0-B7; Later = dependency closure B7, references by dependency hash, cross-host continuation transport, global distribution); section 3 (named AST, executable image with :load-bound/:load-free and arity closures, projection merged and dormant and lossy: 1.0 -> 1, NFC, no tail flags or param names; H soundness: same H implies alpha-equivalent and equal